"use strict";
/* Comprehensive source-linked surgery cloze pack: three atomic cards per teaching block. Generated as literal objects; existing IDs and cards are untouched. (Per-operative-step cards were removed by request.) */
window.SURGERY.cards.push(
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b01-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the arch and its branches","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the arch and its branches","text":"Working from proximal to distal, three vessels normally arise from the arch in this order: the {{c1::innominate artery}} (also called the brachiocephalic artery), which itself divides into the right subclavian and right common carotid arteries; the left common carotid artery; and the left subclavian artery."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b01-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the arch and its branches","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the arch and its branches","text":"A {{c1::bovine arch}} — present in roughly 15 to 25 percent of people — is not actually a bovine pattern at all but a common human variant in which the innominate and left common carotid arteries share a common origin (or the left common carotid arises directly off the innominate) rather than arising as two separate branches; it matters for surgical planning and for endovascular landing zones but is not itself pathological."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b01-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the arch and its branches","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the arch and its branches","text":"An {{c1::aberrant right subclavian artery (arteria lusoria)}} arises as the most distal branch off the arch rather than from the innominate, and crosses behind the esophagus to reach the right arm; it can cause dysphagia from esophageal compression (dysphagia lusoria) and is often associated with a Kommerell diverticulum, an outpouching at its origin that itself can dilate, dissect, or rupture."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b02-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why arch disease is a brain problem","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why arch disease is a brain problem","text":"The {{c1::circle of Willis}} provides some cross-perfusion between the carotid and vertebrobasilar territories, which is exploited by some cerebral protection strategies, but it is anatomically incomplete or asymmetric in a substantial fraction of people, so it cannot be relied upon uncritically."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b02-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why arch disease is a brain problem","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why arch disease is a brain problem","text":"The brain tolerates remarkably little interruption of its blood supply, and unlike most organs it cannot be protected simply by clamping proximal to it and perfusing everything else, because clamping proximal to the {{c1::arch}} vessels would starve the brain entirely."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b02-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why arch disease is a brain problem","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why arch disease is a brain problem","text":"{{c1::Arch}} pathology comes in the same basic forms as elsewhere in the aorta — degenerative aneurysm, acute or chronic dissection extending from the ascending aorta into and through the arch, and diffuse atherosclerotic disease of a heavily calcified, friable \"shaggy\" aorta that is itself a source of embolic stroke — but because the arch cannot simply be cross-clamped and bypassed the way a segment of leg artery can, every one of these processes forces the same fundamental surgical problem: how do you work on a vessel that is also the sole conduit to the brain?"},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b03-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","text":"{{c1::Isolated arch}} aneurysms, like root aneurysms, are usually asymptomatic and found on imaging performed for another reason; when symptomatic, chest, back or interscapular pain suggests expansion, impending rupture, or dissection."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b03-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","text":"Indications mirror the {{c1::root}} and ascending aorta: a degenerative arch aneurysm is generally repaired around 5.5 cm (lower with connective-tissue disease, rapid growth, or family history of dissection, exactly as for the root), while acute type A dissection extending into the arch is a surgical emergency regardless of diameter because of the risk of rupture, malperfusion and death."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b03-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","text":"Chronic dissection with a progressively enlarging false lumen, or extensive disease spanning from the {{c1::ascending aorta}} through the arch into the descending aorta, are the settings where the surgeon must decide up front between a limited hemiarch and a more extensive total arch, possibly combined with a staged descending repair."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b04-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Why the distal anastomosis must be done open","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Why the distal anastomosis must be done open","text":"The solution is to perform the {{c1::distal anastomosis open}} — with no clamp on the arch at all — which means the surgeon needs a completely still, bloodless, unpressurized field to sew into, and the only way to achieve that without a clamp is to temporarily stop the circulation to the whole body."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b04-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Why the distal anastomosis must be done open","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Why the distal anastomosis must be done open","text":"This is {{c1::circulatory arrest}}: pump flow is stopped entirely for a defined period while the arch is opened, the head vessels are inspected and managed, and the distal graft anastomosis is completed under direct vision."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b04-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Why the distal anastomosis must be done open","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Why the distal anastomosis must be done open","text":"In routine aortic surgery below the {{c1::arch}}, the surgeon isolates the segment to be replaced with a cross-clamp, works within a bloodless field distal to the clamp, and the rest of the body continues to be perfused normally by the bypass machine through an unobstructed path."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b05-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest and the safe-time relationship","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest and the safe-time relationship","text":"Stopping all blood flow to the brain is tolerated only because cooling dramatically slows cellular metabolism, and the classical technique — {{c1::deep hypothermic circulatory arrest (DHCA)}} — cools the whole patient via the cardiopulmonary bypass heat exchanger to a core temperature of roughly 18 to 20 degrees Celsius before flow is stopped."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b05-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest and the safe-time relationship","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest and the safe-time relationship","text":"At 18 degrees, the classically quoted \"safe\" arrest time before the risk of neurologic injury rises appreciably is on the order of {{c1::30 to 40 minutes}}; beyond that window, the incidence of stroke and, even more so, of transient neurologic dysfunction climbs progressively."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b05-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest and the safe-time relationship","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Deep hypothermic circulatory arrest and the safe-time relationship","text":"This has driven the modern shift toward {{c1::moderate hypothermia}} (roughly 24 to 28 degrees) combined with active cerebral perfusion during the arrest interval: because the brain is being separately perfused rather than relying solely on cold-induced metabolic suppression, the body does not need to be cooled as deeply, shortening bypass time while still protecting the brain — and often the rest of the body as well, since moderate hypothermia still affords some systemic protection during the brief periods when even the cerebral perfusion is interrupted."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b06-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Cerebral protection strategies — antegrade and retrograde perfusion, and NIRS","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Cerebral protection strategies — antegrade and retrograde perfusion, and NIRS","text":"{{c1::Retrograde cerebral perfusion (RCP)}} takes the opposite approach: cold oxygenated blood is infused backward through a cannula in the superior vena cava, flowing retrograde through the cerebral venous system."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b06-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Cerebral protection strategies — antegrade and retrograde perfusion, and NIRS","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Cerebral protection strategies — antegrade and retrograde perfusion, and NIRS","text":"{{c1::Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS)}} cerebral oximetry is the standard real-time monitor during arch surgery: sensors on the forehead estimate regional cerebral oxygen saturation non-invasively."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b06-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Cerebral protection strategies — antegrade and retrograde perfusion, and NIRS","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Cerebral protection strategies — antegrade and retrograde perfusion, and NIRS","text":"It provides some hypothermic protection and may help wash embolic debris out of the {{c1::cerebral circulation}}, but it does not deliver nutritive, physiologic antegrade flow to brain tissue the way ACP does, and it has been largely supplanted by ACP as the primary strategy for anything beyond short arrest times — RCP is now used mainly as an adjunct or a fallback when antegrade cannulation is not feasible."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b07-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation — hemiarch versus total arch","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"The operation — hemiarch versus total arch","text":"A {{c1::hemiarch}} replacement addresses only the undersurface (lesser curvature) of the arch immediately adjacent to the ascending aorta, without touching the origins of any of the three head vessels; the open distal anastomosis is fashioned just proximal to the innominate artery."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b07-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation — hemiarch versus total arch","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"The operation — hemiarch versus total arch","text":"A {{c1::total arch}} replacement is required when disease involves the origins of the head vessels themselves, or when the entire arch is aneurysmal."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b07-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation — hemiarch versus total arch","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"The operation — hemiarch versus total arch","text":"The {{c1::island technique}} leaves the three ostia attached to a single common patch (\"island\") of native aortic wall, which is then sewn as one unit into a matching opening in the graft — technically simpler and faster, but it leaves a broad island of potentially diseased native tissue in the repair and reperfuses all three vessels simultaneously."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b08-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Extending the repair — elephant trunk and frozen elephant trunk","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Extending the repair — elephant trunk and frozen elephant trunk","text":"The classic {{c1::(open) elephant trunk}} technique solves this by staging the repair: at the first operation, the ascending aorta and arch are replaced in the usual way, but instead of completing the distal anastomosis to normal descending aorta, a segment of graft is left free-floating, unattached, hanging down into the descending aorta like a trunk."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b08-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Extending the repair — elephant trunk and frozen elephant trunk","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Extending the repair — elephant trunk and frozen elephant trunk","text":"The {{c1::frozen elephant trunk (FET)}} is the modern hybrid evolution: instead of leaving a floppy free graft segment for a separate later operation, a self-expanding covered stent-graft is deployed antegrade into the proximal descending aorta at the time of the arch operation itself, sewn into the same distal anastomosis that completes the arch graft."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b08-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Extending the repair — elephant trunk and frozen elephant trunk","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Extending the repair — elephant trunk and frozen elephant trunk","text":"When disease extends well beyond the {{c1::arch}} into the descending thoracic aorta, replacing everything from the root to the mid-descending aorta in a single operation under one long period of circulatory arrest is often more than a patient can safely tolerate."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b09-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"{{c1::Spinal cord ischemia and paraplegia}} become a real risk whenever the repair extends well into the descending thoracic aorta, because doing so sacrifices segmental intercostal arteries that can supply the anterior spinal artery, including the dominant artery of Adamkiewicz in some patients."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b09-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"The {{c1::left recurrent laryngeal nerve}}, which loops posteriorly around the aortic arch near the ligamentum arteriosum, and the phrenic nerve, which runs along the pericardium in the operative field, are both at risk of direct injury during arch mobilization — RLN injury presents as hoarseness and vocal cord paresis, phrenic injury as hemidiaphragm paralysis. Bleeding and coagulopathy after prolonged bypass and circulatory arrest reflect both dilutional and hypothermia-induced platelet and clotting-factor dysfunction, compounded by the multiple graft and reimplantation suture lines of a total arch; management is rewarming, factor and platelet correction, and re-exploration for a persistent surgical source."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b09-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"Risk is reduced by cerebrospinal fluid drainage ({{c1::lowering CSF pressure}} to preserve spinal cord perfusion pressure), maintaining an adequate mean arterial pressure post-operatively, minimizing the length of aorta covered, and staging extensive repairs (exactly the rationale behind the elephant trunk strategies) rather than covering the entire thoracic aorta in one sitting."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b10-01","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — zones, numbers and named strategies","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — zones, numbers and named strategies","text":"Know the {{c1::Ishimaru zones}} (0 through the ascending aorta/arch, extending distally through the descending aorta) as the vocabulary for describing disease extent and planning hemiarch versus total arch versus staged descending repair."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b10-02","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — zones, numbers and named strategies","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — zones, numbers and named strategies","text":"Know the branch order of the arch cold — {{c1::innominate, then left common carotid, then left subclavian}} — and the two classic variants: a bovine arch (shared innominate/left carotid origin, present in roughly 15 to 25 percent of people) and an aberrant right subclavian artery with its associated Kommerell diverticulum."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-arch-b10-03","section":"arch","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — zones, numbers and named strategies","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — zones, numbers and named strategies","text":"Know why a clampless, open {{c1::distal anastomosis}} is required at all — a diseased arch cannot be safely clamped without either fracturing atheroma or occluding cerebral flow — and that circulatory arrest exists specifically to create a bloodless field for that anastomosis."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b01-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the aortic root you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the aortic root you forgot","text":"The {{c1::annulus}} is the fibrous, scalloped ring at the base of the root where the three aortic valve leaflets actually insert — it is not a flat circle but a crown-shaped structure, with each leaflet's attachment rising and falling like a coronet."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b01-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the aortic root you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the aortic root you forgot","text":"Above the annulus are the three {{c1::sinuses of Valsalva}} — right, left and non-coronary — each a bulge in the aortic wall that sits behind a valve leaflet."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b01-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the aortic root you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the aortic root you forgot","text":"Above the sinuses, where the bulges taper back to the calibre of the tubular aorta, is the {{c1::sinotubular junction (STJ)}}, a distinct anatomic ridge that is just as important to valve function as the annulus."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b02-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — annuloaortic ectasia and functional regurgitation","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — annuloaortic ectasia and functional regurgitation","text":"The classic root disease is {{c1::annuloaortic ectasia}}: symmetric, fusiform dilation of the annulus, sinuses and proximal ascending aorta together, producing the pear- or flask-shaped root seen on imaging."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b02-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — annuloaortic ectasia and functional regurgitation","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — annuloaortic ectasia and functional regurgitation","text":"In {{c1::Marfan syndrome}}, a mutation in the fibrillin-1 gene weakens the microfibrillar scaffold of the aortic media and dysregulates TGF-beta signalling, leading to progressive elastic-fibre fragmentation and cystic medial degeneration."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b02-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — annuloaortic ectasia and functional regurgitation","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — annuloaortic ectasia and functional regurgitation","text":"In {{c1::Loeys-Dietz syndrome}}, mutations affecting the TGF-beta receptor pathway (TGFBR1/2 and related genes) produce an even more aggressive aortopathy, with dissection occurring at smaller diameters and a tendency toward arterial tortuosity and aneurysms beyond the aortic root."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b03-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","text":"Most are found incidentally on imaging obtained for another reason, on a murmur of aortic regurgitation heard at a routine visit, or through {{c1::family screening}} once a relative is diagnosed with Marfan or another heritable aortic disease."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b03-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","text":"{{c1::Symptomatic presentation}} is a late or catastrophic finding — chest or back pain suggests dissection or impending rupture, and progressive dyspnoea or orthopnoea reflects decompensating severe AR."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b03-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications for surgery","text":"Because the disease is {{c1::silent until it isn't}}, imaging surveillance and threshold-based elective surgery are what actually save these patients' lives; waiting for symptoms means waiting too long."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b04-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation, step by step — the modified Bentall","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — the modified Bentall","text":"The modern Bentall operation replaces the annulus, sinuses and valve with a single prefabricated {{c1::composite valve-graft}} — a prosthetic valve (mechanical or biologic) sewn into a Dacron tube — and reimplants both coronary arteries into that tube as buttons of native aortic wall."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b04-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation, step by step — the modified Bentall","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — the modified Bentall","text":"The critical, unforgiving step follows: each {{c1::coronary ostium}} is mobilized as a button — a circular island of native aortic wall roughly 5 to 8 mm around the ostium — leaving enough surrounding tissue to sew without tension but not so much redundant tissue that the button will kink or fold when reimplanted."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b04-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation, step by step — the modified Bentall","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — the modified Bentall","text":"The patient is positioned supine for a {{c1::median sternotomy}}, systemically heparinized, and placed on cardiopulmonary bypass (arterial cannulation typically distal ascending aorta or axillary artery if the aorta is diseased near the planned cannulation site; venous cannulation as the concomitant procedure dictates)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b05-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Valve-sparing root replacement — David versus Yacoub","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Valve-sparing root replacement — David versus Yacoub","text":"In a young patient with structurally normal, pliable, trileaflet (or a repairable bicuspid) leaflets whose regurgitation is purely functional from annular and STJ widening, replacing a perfectly good native valve with a prosthesis trades one problem (root aneurysm) for a lifetime of prosthesis-related risk (anticoagulation, structural deterioration, endocarditis risk). {{c1::Valve-sparing root replacement}} avoids that trade by replacing only the diseased aortic wall around the valve and preserving the native leaflets, restoring the geometry that lets them coapt again."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b05-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Valve-sparing root replacement — David versus Yacoub","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Valve-sparing root replacement — David versus Yacoub","text":"The {{c1::David procedure (reimplantation technique)}} is the modern default."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b05-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Valve-sparing root replacement — David versus Yacoub","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Valve-sparing root replacement — David versus Yacoub","text":"The {{c1::Yacoub procedure (remodeling technique)}} is the older approach: the graft is scalloped into three tongues that recreate neo-sinuses and are sewn to the top of the excised native sinuses, with the coronary buttons reimplanted into the scallops — but the annulus itself is not reinforced or resuspended."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b06-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Bailouts and alternative conduits — Cabrol shunt, homograft and Ross","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Bailouts and alternative conduits — Cabrol shunt, homograft and Ross","text":"The bailout is the {{c1::Cabrol shunt (Cabrol technique)}}: instead of implanting the button directly, an interposition tube graft (or a small side conduit) connects the coronary ostia to the side of the main aortic graft, relieving tension by giving the coronary blood a longer, unconstrained path to travel."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b06-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Bailouts and alternative conduits — Cabrol shunt, homograft and Ross","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Bailouts and alternative conduits — Cabrol shunt, homograft and Ross","text":"When the operation is for {{c1::infective endocarditis with a root or annular abscess}}, the choice of conduit changes."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b06-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Bailouts and alternative conduits — Cabrol shunt, homograft and Ross","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Bailouts and alternative conduits — Cabrol shunt, homograft and Ross","text":"Prosthetic material (mechanical valve, Dacron graft) sits in a field that has already proven susceptible to infection, so many surgeons prefer a {{c1::cryopreserved aortic homograft}} (allograft) root, which resists reinfection better than prosthetic material, requires no long-term anticoagulation, and can be tailored to reconstruct destroyed annular tissue."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b07-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"Late, a weak point at the button-to-graft suture line can form a {{c1::coronary button pseudoaneurysm}}, often found incidentally on surveillance CT years later and treated with reintervention (open repair, covered stent, or reoperation) once identified, because it carries a rupture risk."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b07-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"Beyond the buttons, generalized {{c1::hemorrhage}} from the multiple suture lines after a long bypass and cross-clamp time reflects both surgical bleeding and dilutional/consumptive coagulopathy; management follows the same principles as any post-bypass bleeding — protamine reversal, correction of coagulation factors and platelets, warming, and re-exploration for a surgical source that does not respond. Valve or conduit thrombosis is a mechanical-valve-specific risk, more likely with sub-therapeutic anticoagulation or a low-flow state, presenting with new murmur change, heart failure or embolic events, and confirmed with echocardiography (fluoroscopy for leaflet motion); management ranges from thrombolysis to emergency reoperation depending on severity."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b07-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"{{c1::Tamponade}} from a contained mediastinal collection and low cardiac output from myocardial stunning after a long cross-clamp time are managed with the same general post-cardiotomy principles as any open-heart case."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b08-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Anticoagulation and long-term implications of the conduit choice","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation and long-term implications of the conduit choice","text":"A {{c1::mechanical composite valve-graft}} requires lifelong warfarin anticoagulation — typically targeting an INR in the range of 2.5 to 3.5 for an aortic mechanical valve, often with low-dose aspirin added — because mechanical leaflets are thrombogenic surfaces and thrombosis or embolism is a lifelong risk without adequate anticoagulation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b08-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Anticoagulation and long-term implications of the conduit choice","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation and long-term implications of the conduit choice","text":"A {{c1::biologic (bioprosthetic) composite valve-graft}} avoids the need for lifelong warfarin — patients typically need only a brief period of anticoagulation or antiplatelet therapy after surgery — but the tissue valve undergoes gradual structural deterioration, with a meaningful risk of needing reoperation or transcatheter valve-in-valve intervention within one to two decades, faster in younger patients whose calcium metabolism accelerates tissue degeneration."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b08-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Anticoagulation and long-term implications of the conduit choice","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation and long-term implications of the conduit choice","text":"The choice between a mechanical and a biologic ({{c1::bioprosthetic}}, tissue) valve within the composite conduit carries the same trade-offs as an isolated aortic valve replacement, but the consequences are magnified because the entire root and coronary reimplantation are on the line if the valve later needs replacing."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b09-01","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named techniques and connective-tissue disease","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named techniques and connective-tissue disease","text":"Know why endocarditis with root/annular abscess pushes toward a {{c1::homograft}} rather than prosthetic material — resistance to reinfection — and why connective-tissue disease patients need lifelong downstream aortic surveillance after root replacement, since the rest of their aorta (arch, descending, abdominal) remains at elevated dissection risk from the same underlying wall defect even after the root itself has been fixed."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b09-02","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named techniques and connective-tissue disease","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named techniques and connective-tissue disease","text":"Root replacement techniques by what happens to the annulus and valve: {{c1::Bentall}} = valve replaced, in a graft, buttons sewn direct. David = valve spared, annulus captured inside the graft. Yacoub = valve spared, annulus left free. Cabrol = a bailout conduit, not a root-replacement technique in itself. Ross = valve replaced by the patient's own pulmonary valve, pulmonary valve replaced by a homograft."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bentall-b09-03","section":"bentall","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named techniques and connective-tissue disease","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named techniques and connective-tissue disease","text":"At the ceiling you should be able to rattle off the size thresholds cold: degenerative aneurysm at {{c1::5.5 cm}}; Marfan syndrome at 5.0 cm (4.5 cm with risk factors such as family history of dissection, growth >=0.3 cm/year, or planned pregnancy); Loeys-Dietz syndrome operated at smaller diameters still (often 4.0 to 4.5 cm) because it dissects at smaller sizes than Marfan; bicuspid aortic valve with a root phenotype at 5.5 cm (5.0 cm with risk factors); and a lower threshold (~4.5 cm) whenever the patient is already having the aortic valve replaced for other reasons."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b01-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Start here — what the bypass machine actually replaces","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what the bypass machine actually replaces","text":"The pump replaces the left ventricle: it generates the {{c1::flow that perfuses the body}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b01-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Start here — what the bypass machine actually replaces","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what the bypass machine actually replaces","text":"The {{c1::oxygenator}} replaces the lungs: it is where gas exchange happens."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b01-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Start here — what the bypass machine actually replaces","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what the bypass machine actually replaces","text":"The single most important conceptual split: the {{c1::pump makes flow}} (it replaces the LV) and the oxygenator makes gas exchange (it replaces the lungs)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b02-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"The circuit, component by component","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"The circuit, component by component","text":"Blood leaves the patient through a {{c1::venous cannula}} placed in the right atrium or the two venae cavae."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b02-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"The circuit, component by component","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"The circuit, component by component","text":"It is not pumped out — it drains by {{c1::gravity siphon}} (or with gentle vacuum assist) down into a venous reservoir, a soft or hard chamber that sits below the level of the heart and acts as a buffer of volume."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b02-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"The circuit, component by component","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"The circuit, component by component","text":"From the reservoir a {{c1::main pump}} — either a roller pump that squeezes the tubing or a centrifugal pump that spins blood outward by a cone or impeller — drives the blood forward."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b03-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Physiology on bypass — flow, pressure and oxygen delivery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Physiology on bypass — flow, pressure and oxygen delivery","text":"The commonest error a trainee makes on bypass is to treat {{c1::mean arterial pressure (MAP)}} as the goal."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b03-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Physiology on bypass — flow, pressure and oxygen delivery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Physiology on bypass — flow, pressure and oxygen delivery","text":"The goal of perfusion is adequate {{c1::oxygen delivery (DO2)}} to the tissues, and MAP is only one visible number among the several that determine it."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b03-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Physiology on bypass — flow, pressure and oxygen delivery","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Physiology on bypass — flow, pressure and oxygen delivery","text":"Oxygen delivery is the product of blood flow and the oxygen content of that blood: {{c1::DO2 = pump flow x arterial oxygen content}}, where oxygen content is dominated by haemoglobin concentration and its saturation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b04-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Anticoagulation — heparin, the ACT, and the HIT problem","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation — heparin, the ACT, and the HIT problem","text":"The standard agent is unfractionated {{c1::heparin}}, given as a weight-based bolus of roughly 300 to 400 units per kilogram, and its effect is confirmed not by a clotting time from the lab but at the bedside by the activated clotting time (ACT)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b04-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Anticoagulation — heparin, the ACT, and the HIT problem","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation — heparin, the ACT, and the HIT problem","text":"A safe ACT for initiating and running CPB is generally taken as {{c1::greater than 400 to 480 seconds}} (many programmes use 480), rechecked at intervals through the case with supplemental heparin as needed, because heparin responsiveness varies widely between patients."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b04-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Anticoagulation — heparin, the ACT, and the HIT problem","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation — heparin, the ACT, and the HIT problem","text":"Some patients are relatively {{c1::heparin resistant}} and fail to reach target ACT despite large doses; the usual cause is antithrombin deficiency (heparin works by potentiating antithrombin), corrected by giving antithrombin concentrate or fresh frozen plasma."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b05-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Cannulation — getting blood safely out and back in","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Cannulation — getting blood safely out and back in","text":"The {{c1::arterial cannula}} is placed first, conventionally into a purse-string on healthy, non-calcified distal ascending aorta just proximal to the innominate artery, positioned so its jet points down the arch and not at the aortic wall or a great-vessel origin."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b05-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Cannulation — getting blood safely out and back in","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Cannulation — getting blood safely out and back in","text":"When the ascending aorta is heavily diseased, dissected, or in reoperations, alternative inflow sites are chosen — the {{c1::axillary (subclavian) artery}} gives antegrade true-lumen flow and is favoured in acute type A dissection, while femoral cannulation gives rapid access but delivers retrograde flow that can perfuse a false lumen or shower atheroma."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b05-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Cannulation — getting blood safely out and back in","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Cannulation — getting blood safely out and back in","text":"The moment flow begins through a new arterial cannula, the surgeon and perfusionist watch three things: a reasonable and pulsatile-then-flat {{c1::line pressure}}, a soft (not tense, not blue) aorta, and no sudden loss of the arterial trace."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b06-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Going on bypass — the controlled handoff","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Going on bypass — the controlled handoff","text":"Over the first tens of seconds the team is watching a coordinated set of signals: the arterial {{c1::line pressure}} should be reasonable rather than sharply high; the patient's MAP should be supported as native ejection falls; the venous return should be brisk and the reservoir level stable; the face and sclerae should stay pink and symmetric (unilateral facial plethora or a fall in a single-arm pressure suggests innominate or false-lumen malperfusion); and the heart should empty and decompress as the machine takes over its work."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b06-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Going on bypass — the controlled handoff","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Going on bypass — the controlled handoff","text":"If an intracardiac or aortic procedure requires a still, protected heart, the surgeon places a {{c1::cross-clamp}} on the ascending aorta between the arterial cannula and the aortic valve, isolating the coronary circulation, and delivers cardioplegia to arrest and protect the myocardium (covered in the next block)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b06-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Going on bypass — the controlled handoff","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Going on bypass — the controlled handoff","text":"A high arterial line pressure with a tense, discolouring aorta at this stage is an emergency signal of malposition or {{c1::dissection}} and demands immediate cessation of flow and reassessment."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b07-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Cross-clamp and cardioplegia — protecting the arrested heart","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Cross-clamp and cardioplegia — protecting the arrested heart","text":"This is achieved with {{c1::cardioplegia}}: a solution, typically high in potassium, that arrests the heart in diastole by depolarizing and holding the myocyte membrane, stopping electrical and mechanical activity and thereby collapsing the heart's oxygen demand."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b07-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Cross-clamp and cardioplegia — protecting the arrested heart","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Cross-clamp and cardioplegia — protecting the arrested heart","text":"Cardioplegia is delivered {{c1::antegrade}} down the aortic root (into the coronary ostia, provided the aortic valve is competent so the solution does not simply wash into the left ventricle) or directly into the coronary ostia when the aorta is open, and/or retrograde through a catheter in the coronary sinus, which perfuses the myocardium backwards through the venous system and is valuable when severe coronary stenoses would prevent even antegrade distribution or when the aortic valve is incompetent."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b07-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Cross-clamp and cardioplegia — protecting the arrested heart","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Cross-clamp and cardioplegia — protecting the arrested heart","text":"Composition varies by institution: {{c1::blood cardioplegia}} (commonly a 4:1 blood-to-crystalloid mix, warm or cold, sometimes given as an initial induction and a terminal warm \"hot shot\") and single-dose crystalloid formulas such as del Nido and HTK (Custodiol) that allow long intervals between doses."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b08-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Coming off bypass — the prerequisites and the trial off","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Coming off bypass — the prerequisites and the trial off","text":"Only with the prerequisites met does the surgeon perform a {{c1::dynamic trial off bypass}}: venous drainage is progressively reduced and pump flow lowered in steps, allowing the heart to fill and take over ejection while the team watches biventricular filling and contractility on TEE, the arterial and pulmonary pressures, and the surgical result of the repair."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b08-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Coming off bypass — the prerequisites and the trial off","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Coming off bypass — the prerequisites and the trial off","text":"Prerequisites before the trial off — \"{{c1::warm, pink, beating, full, clean}}\": warm (normothermic), pink (ventilated and oxygenating), beating (a perfusing, paced-if-needed rhythm), full (electrolytes, haematocrit and volume corrected), clean (de-aired on TEE with a satisfactory surgical repair)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b08-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Coming off bypass — the prerequisites and the trial off","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Coming off bypass — the prerequisites and the trial off","text":"Separation from bypass fails far more often because a reversible prerequisite was skipped than because the heart is truly unable to work, so the discipline of coming off is a {{c1::checklist done before the trial}}, not a set of drugs given during it."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b09-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Failure to separate — name the phenotype before you escalate","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Failure to separate — name the phenotype before you escalate","text":"The correct discipline is to {{c1::name the phenotype}} using coupled information from TEE and the pressure monitors, because each phenotype has a different first move."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b09-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Failure to separate — name the phenotype before you escalate","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Failure to separate — name the phenotype before you escalate","text":"The six causes are, usefully, a checklist: {{c1::preload}} (the heart is under- or over-filled), pump/contractility (LV failure — ischaemia, stunning, infarct, poor protection), right ventricle (RV failure from air or ischaemia in the RCA territory, high pulmonary afterload, or protamine), rhythm (bradycardia, heart block, atrial fibrillation losing atrial kick), afterload (vasoplegia with a low SVR, or, less commonly, excessive afterload), and a mechanical or surgical cause (a residual valve lesion, an obstructed graft, dynamic LV outflow obstruction, tamponade physiology, or an unrecognized structural problem)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b09-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Failure to separate — name the phenotype before you escalate","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Failure to separate — name the phenotype before you escalate","text":"Contractile failure is supported with {{c1::inotropes}} and, where they are insufficient, an intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) for the failing LV."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b10-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Protamine and decannulation — the last hazards","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Protamine and decannulation — the last hazards","text":"The antidote is {{c1::protamine}}, a positively charged protein that binds and neutralizes heparin, dosed roughly at 1 mg per 100 units of the total heparin given and titrated to return the ACT to baseline."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b10-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Protamine and decannulation — the last hazards","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Protamine and decannulation — the last hazards","text":"{{c1::Protamine}} is given slowly and only after communication with the perfusionist, because the pump suckers must be turned off (protamine in the circuit will clot it) and because protamine itself can cause dangerous reactions."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b10-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Protamine and decannulation — the last hazards","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Protamine and decannulation — the last hazards","text":"Reactions are classically grouped as {{c1::Type I}}, a rate-related systemic hypotension from vasodilation that responds to slowing or stopping the infusion; Type II, true anaphylactic or anaphylactoid reactions (higher risk in patients previously exposed, including those on NPH insulin or with fish allergy, and after vasectomy); and the feared Type III, catastrophic pulmonary vasoconstriction with acute right heart failure and systemic collapse."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b11-01","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, formulas and named concepts","partId":"block:11","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":10,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, formulas and named concepts","text":"At senior level you are expected to reason quantitatively about perfusion. {{c1::Oxygen delivery on bypass}} is DO2 = pump flow x arterial oxygen content, where content is approximately (1.34 x haemoglobin x saturation) plus a small dissolved term; the practical message is that flow and haemoglobin, not MAP, set delivery."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b11-02","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, formulas and named concepts","partId":"block:11","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":10,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, formulas and named concepts","text":"The critical indexed threshold below which anaerobic metabolism and AKI risk climb is a {{c1::DO2i of roughly 270 mL/min/m2}}, the target of goal-directed perfusion."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-bypass-b11-03","section":"bypass","category":"foundational","topic":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, formulas and named concepts","partId":"block:11","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":10,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, formulas and named concepts","text":"Know the {{c1::temperature and neuroprotection}} vocabulary: for aortic arch work requiring an open distal anastomosis, the circulation may be stopped entirely under deep hypothermic circulatory arrest (DHCA), usually combined with antegrade or retrograde cerebral perfusion and monitored with near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) cerebral oximetry; hypothermia buys ischaemic time by lowering the cerebral metabolic rate."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b01-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Start here — the coronary anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the coronary anatomy you forgot","text":"The {{c1::left main coronary artery}} is short — often only a centimetre or two — and almost immediately bifurcates (sometimes trifurcates, with an intervening ramus branch) into the left anterior descending (LAD) artery, which runs down the anterior interventricular groove to supply the anterior wall, the anteroseptal septum, and the apex, and the left circumflex (LCx) artery, which runs in the left atrioventricular groove around the obtuse margin to supply the lateral wall."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b01-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Start here — the coronary anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the coronary anatomy you forgot","text":"The {{c1::right coronary artery (RCA)}} runs in the right atrioventricular groove and, in most people, continues around the inferior heart to become the posterior descending artery (PDA), which runs in the posterior interventricular groove."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b01-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Start here — the coronary anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the coronary anatomy you forgot","text":"Whichever artery gives rise to the PDA defines {{c1::coronary dominance}}: right-dominant (from the RCA) in roughly 80 to 85 percent of people, left-dominant (from the LCx) in a minority, and codominant in the rest."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b02-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the LAD gets the LIMA","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the LAD gets the LIMA","text":"Two separate biological facts converge to make the {{c1::left internal mammary artery (LIMA) to LAD}} graft the single most important anastomosis in cardiac surgery."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b02-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the LAD gets the LIMA","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the LAD gets the LIMA","text":"Second, the {{c1::LIMA}} is not just conduit — it is a muscular artery harvested in continuity with its native origin off the subclavian artery, so unlike a vein graft it retains an endothelium and vasoreactivity biologically similar to the coronary artery it is sewn to, resists intimal hyperplasia and atherosclerosis far better than vein, and has been shown repeatedly to remain patent (open and functioning) at 10 and 15 years in the great majority of patients."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b02-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the LAD gets the LIMA","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the LAD gets the LIMA","text":"Put together: the highest-value target gets the most durable conduit, and that pairing — LIMA to LAD — is why it is called the {{c1::anchor graft}} of a bypass operation and is placed in essentially every CABG unless the LAD itself is unsuitable."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b03-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Presentation, evidence and the decision to operate","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, evidence and the decision to operate","text":"The decision to operate, rather than stent or treat medically, rests on a mix of anatomic complexity, comorbidity, and increasingly, trial evidence weighed by a multidisciplinary {{c1::Heart Team}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b03-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Presentation, evidence and the decision to operate","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, evidence and the decision to operate","text":"For left main disease specifically, {{c1::EXCEL}} and NOBLE both compared PCI with drug-eluting stents against CABG; taken together with longer-term follow-up, they showed that outcomes are more comparable for lower to intermediate anatomic complexity left main disease, while CABG maintains an edge as anatomic complexity (SYNTAX score) rises — reinforcing that the choice is anatomy- and complexity-dependent rather than one-size-fits-all."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b03-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Presentation, evidence and the decision to operate","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, evidence and the decision to operate","text":"In ischemic cardiomyopathy with a severely reduced ejection fraction, {{c1::STICH}} showed that CABG (with or without surgical ventricular reconstruction) provided a long-term survival benefit over medical therapy alone in patients with ischemic LV dysfunction and coronary anatomy suitable for CABG; the STICH viability substudy did not show that viability testing selected who received this benefit, cementing surgical revascularization as standard in appropriately selected patients with ischemic heart failure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b04-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"Each distal anastomosis is a precise {{c1::end-to-side}} join between the open end of the conduit and a small arteriotomy in the native coronary artery beyond the stenosis."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b04-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The arteriotomy is fashioned longitudinally, and the graft is bevelled to create a {{c1::heel and toe}} — the proximal (heel) and distal (toe) points of the elliptical opening — so that the completed anastomosis is a smooth, non-narrowing continuation of the artery rather than a stepped or kinked junction; a poorly constructed heel or toe is a common technical cause of early graft failure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b04-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"Conduits may be used as simple single grafts (one conduit, one target), as {{c1::sequential grafts}} (one conduit with side-to-side anastomoses to an intermediate target before a final end-to-side anastomosis to the last target, extending the reach of a single length of conduit), or as a composite (Y or T) graft, where a second conduit is anastomosed onto the side of the first (commonly a radial or vein segment off the side of the LIMA) rather than independently off the aorta, reducing the number of proximal aortic anastomoses."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b05-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Conduit choice — the second most important decision after the LIMA","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Conduit choice — the second most important decision after the LIMA","text":"The {{c1::great saphenous vein}} remains the most commonly used conduit for its ease of harvest and abundant length, but as a vein used as an artery it is exposed to arterial pressure it was never built for, and over years it develops intimal hyperplasia and accelerated atherosclerosis, giving it inferior long-term patency compared with arterial conduit."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b05-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Conduit choice — the second most important decision after the LIMA","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Conduit choice — the second most important decision after the LIMA","text":"The {{c1::radial artery}} has become the preferred second conduit for the next most important target in appropriately selected patients: the 2021 ACC/AHA/SCAI guidelines and pooled evidence from the RADIAL meta-analysis (patient-level pooling of the major randomized trials) both support radial artery graft patency and clinical outcomes superior to saphenous vein for grafting a significantly stenosed target, provided the target vessel has a high-grade stenosis (again, competitive flow undermines an arterial graft to a mildly diseased vessel more than it does a vein)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b05-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Conduit choice — the second most important decision after the LIMA","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Conduit choice — the second most important decision after the LIMA","text":"Conduit durability ranking, best to worst for long-term patency — {{c1::LIMA in situ > radial artery (to a high-grade stenosis) > right}} ITA > saphenous vein (conventional) — with no-touch vein harvest narrowing that last gap."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b06-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Target selection, routing and the technical details that matter","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Target selection, routing and the technical details that matter","text":"Once the anastomosis is complete, the conduit must be routed {{c1::without tension, twist, or kink}} on the beating, reperfused, and eventually closing heart — a straight, appropriately lengthed lie from the aorta (or from the LIMA's native origin) to the target, checked by watching the graft as the heart is manipulated and the chest is prepared for closure, because a graft that looks perfect on the arrested, decompressed heart can kink once the heart re-fills and returns to its normal position and size."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b06-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Target selection, routing and the technical details that matter","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Target selection, routing and the technical details that matter","text":"Manipulating the ascending aorta — for cannulation, cross-clamping, side-biting clamping for proximal anastomoses, and any additional partial clamping — carries a real risk of dislodging atheromatous debris into the cerebral or systemic circulation, particularly in a heavily calcified or atheromatous (\"porcelain\" in its most severe form) aorta; this {{c1::embolic risk from aortic manipulation}} is a major driver of perioperative stroke in on-pump CABG and is precisely the risk that off-pump and anaortic techniques (covered in the companion OPCAB article) are designed to reduce."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b06-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Target selection, routing and the technical details that matter","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Target selection, routing and the technical details that matter","text":"A vessel is chosen as a bypass target when it has a {{c1::haemodynamically significant stenosis}}, is of adequate calibre (generally at least about 1.5 mm, though smaller vessels can be grafted by experienced hands), and has reasonable distal runoff — that is, the artery beyond the anastomosis is not itself diffusely diseased down to nothing, because a graft delivering flow into a dead end will fail regardless of how well the anastomosis itself is constructed."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b07-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"Perioperative myocardial infarction after CABG is diagnosed by a combination of new ECG changes, regional wall motion abnormality, and a substantial rise in cardiac biomarkers (troponin) beyond the expected postoperative elevation from the surgery itself, and its most common cause is a technical problem with a specific graft — an occluded or severely stenosed anastomosis, a kinked or twisted conduit, or embolization of air or debris down a coronary, most often the {{c1::right coronary artery}} because of its anterior take-off from the aortic root, which makes it the natural collecting point for air during de-airing."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b07-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"When perioperative MI is suspected, {{c1::urgent coronary angiography}} (or a return to the operating room if angiography is not immediately available and index of suspicion is high) is used to identify and, where possible, revise or re-graft the culprit vessel, because early recognition and correction meaningfully changes outcome."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b07-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"{{c1::Early graft}} failure presents as recurrent angina, ECG changes, or haemodynamic instability in the immediate postoperative period and, as above, is a technical problem until proven otherwise."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b08-01","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Pimping ceiling — the fine print","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — the fine print","text":"Know the numeric thresholds cold: a stenosis is generally considered significant enough to graft at {{c1::greater than 70 percent}} diameter narrowing for most epicardial vessels, and greater than 50 percent for the left main, given the disproportionate myocardial territory it protects."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b08-02","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Pimping ceiling — the fine print","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — the fine print","text":"Know the {{c1::SYNTAX score}} conceptually as a lesion-complexity score (not merely a stenosis-severity score) that incorporates number of lesions, bifurcation involvement, chronic total occlusions, calcification, and tortuosity, and that a higher score progressively favours CABG over PCI for equivalent anatomic disease."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-cabg-b08-03","section":"cabg","category":"coronary","topic":"Pimping ceiling — the fine print","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — the fine print","text":"Know that {{c1::FREEDOM}} is the trial to cite for diabetes plus multivessel disease, that STICH/STICHES is the program to cite for ischemic cardiomyopathy with LVEF 35 percent or less; do not use viability testing as a treatment-effect selector, and that EXCEL and NOBLE are the trials to cite for left main disease specifically, with their combined message being complexity-dependent equipoise rather than a uniform winner."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b01-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Start here — what an LVAD is and what it replaces","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what an LVAD is and what it replaces","text":"It has three implanted parts and one that crosses the skin: an {{c1::inflow cannula}} inserted into the apex of the left ventricle, the pump itself, an outflow graft that is sewn to the ascending aorta, and a percutaneous driveline that exits through the abdominal wall to connect to an external controller and batteries."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b01-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Start here — what an LVAD is and what it replaces","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what an LVAD is and what it replaces","text":"Modern durable devices are {{c1::continuous-flow}} pumps, and the current standard is a centrifugal pump with a fully magnetically levitated rotor (the HeartMate 3), which spins blood continuously rather than ejecting it in beats."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b01-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Start here — what an LVAD is and what it replaces","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what an LVAD is and what it replaces","text":"Fix the plumbing in your head: {{c1::inflow from the LV apex, outflow to the ascending aorta, driveline out}} through the abdominal wall."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b02-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Continuous-flow physiology — a circulation without a pulse","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Continuous-flow physiology — a circulation without a pulse","text":"Because the pump delivers a continuous stream and the native ventricle contributes little, patients often have {{c1::no palpable pulse}} and a narrow pulse pressure, so a standard cuff or palpated blood pressure is unreliable; the mean arterial pressure is measured instead with a Doppler over an artery while a cuff is deflated (the pressure at which flow first becomes audible approximates the mean), and a MAP target of roughly 70 to 90 mmHg is used because chronically high afterload reduces pump flow and raises stroke risk."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b02-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Continuous-flow physiology — a circulation without a pulse","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Continuous-flow physiology — a circulation without a pulse","text":"The pump is {{c1::preload-dependent and afterload-sensitive}}: it can only pump what the left ventricle delivers to it, and it pumps less against a high aortic pressure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b02-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Continuous-flow physiology — a circulation without a pulse","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Continuous-flow physiology — a circulation without a pulse","text":"If the left ventricle is underfilled — from hypovolaemia, bleeding, or, importantly, {{c1::right ventricular failure that fails to deliver blood across the lungs}} to the left heart — the pump can pull the ventricular walls inward against the inflow cannula, a suction event that collapses flow and can trigger arrhythmias, seen as low-flow alarms."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b03-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Indications and the INTERMACS profiles","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Indications and the INTERMACS profiles","text":"The strategy is labelled by intent: {{c1::bridge to transplant}} (supporting a transplant candidate until an organ is available), destination therapy (permanent support in a patient who is not a transplant candidate), and bridge to candidacy or decision (supporting a patient while reversible contraindications, such as pulmonary hypertension or end-organ dysfunction, are reassessed)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b03-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Indications and the INTERMACS profiles","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Indications and the INTERMACS profiles","text":"Patient acuity is described by the {{c1::INTERMACS profiles}}, a seven-level scale of how sick the patient is at the time of implant. Profile 1 is \"crash and burn\" — critical cardiogenic shock with worsening perfusion despite escalating support — and it carries the worst outcomes for durable LVAD implantation; profile 2 is progressive decline on inotropes; profile 3 is stable but inotrope-dependent; and profiles 4 to 7 describe progressively more stable ambulatory heart failure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b03-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Indications and the INTERMACS profiles","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Indications and the INTERMACS profiles","text":"An {{c1::LVAD}} is offered in advanced (stage D) heart failure when a patient remains severely symptomatic despite optimal medical and device therapy."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b04-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Patient selection and the right ventricle that decides survival","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Patient selection and the right ventricle that decides survival","text":"The single most important assessment before implanting an LVAD is the {{c1::right ventricle}}, because right ventricular failure after LVAD implantation is a leading cause of early mortality."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b04-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Patient selection and the right ventricle that decides survival","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Patient selection and the right ventricle that decides survival","text":"{{c1::Right ventricular failure}} is the leading cause of early death after LVAD implantation. Assess the RV and pulmonary circulation meticulously before surgery, avoid over-speeding the pump (which shifts the septum and worsens RV function), support the RV with inotropes and pulmonary vasodilators, and be prepared for temporary or durable right-sided mechanical support."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b04-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Patient selection and the right ventricle that decides survival","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Patient selection and the right ventricle that decides survival","text":"The reason is a physiological trap: the LVAD unloads the {{c1::left ventricle}} and increases venous return and flow through the lungs, suddenly increasing the volume the right ventricle must pump; at the same time, over-decompressing the LV can shift the interventricular septum leftward, which paradoxically reduces the septum's contribution to right ventricular contraction."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b05-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"After the pericardium is opened and bypass established, the surgeon selects and prepares the {{c1::apex of the left ventricle}}, sews a fixation ring to it, and cores a circular plug of myocardium from the apex, inspecting the cavity to remove any thrombus and to ensure the inflow will point toward the mitral valve rather than into the septum or free wall."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b05-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The {{c1::inflow cannula of the pump}} is inserted into the apex and secured to the ring, seating the pump against the heart."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b05-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The {{c1::outflow graft}} is then sewn end-to-side to the ascending aorta (with a partial-occlusion clamp), and the driveline is tunnelled through the abdominal wall to exit the skin, positioned to reduce infection risk."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b06-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Anticoagulation and the bleeding paradox","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation and the bleeding paradox","text":"On one hand the {{c1::pump surfaces and the high shear}} are thrombogenic, so patients require anticoagulation with warfarin (to a device-specific INR target, commonly around 2.0 to 3.0) usually combined with aspirin, to prevent pump thrombosis and stroke."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b06-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Anticoagulation and the bleeding paradox","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation and the bleeding paradox","text":"On the other hand, the same high shear across the rotor destroys the largest multimers of {{c1::von Willebrand factor}}, producing an acquired von Willebrand syndrome that predisposes to bleeding, and the non-pulsatile flow promotes the development of gastrointestinal arteriovenous malformations (angiodysplasia) that bleed."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b06-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Anticoagulation and the bleeding paradox","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation and the bleeding paradox","text":"The result is a patient who is simultaneously prone to clotting the pump and to bleeding, most characteristically recurrent {{c1::gastrointestinal bleeding}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b07-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Complications and how to recognise them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to recognise them","text":"Two further problems relate to flow and the valves. {{c1::Suction events}} occur when the pump over-drains an underfilled ventricle and pulls the wall onto the inflow cannula, causing low-flow alarms and arrhythmias, and are managed by treating the cause of underfilling and reducing speed."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b07-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Complications and how to recognise them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to recognise them","text":"Over years, continuous flow with an aortic valve that rarely opens can lead to {{c1::aortic valve commissural fusion and de novo aortic insufficiency}}, which loops regurgitant flow back through the pump and reduces effective forward output."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b07-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Complications and how to recognise them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to recognise them","text":"Across all of these, the recurring lesson is that an LVAD patient with new symptoms needs a systematic assessment of {{c1::pump parameters}} (flow, speed, power, pulsatility index), volume status, the right ventricle, and the valves, not a reflex assumption."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b08-01","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Pimping ceiling — trials, device generations and the transplant question","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — trials, device generations and the transplant question","text":"The pivotal modern trial is {{c1::MOMENTUM 3}}, which compared the fully magnetically levitated centrifugal HeartMate 3 with the older axial-flow HeartMate II and showed markedly less pump thrombosis and stroke and better overall survival free of disabling stroke or reoperation to replace the pump, establishing the magnetically levitated centrifugal device as the standard of care."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b08-02","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Pimping ceiling — trials, device generations and the transplant question","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — trials, device generations and the transplant question","text":"Know the {{c1::INTERMACS}} profiles (1 is crash-and-burn with the worst outcomes), the strategy labels (bridge to transplant, destination therapy, bridge to candidacy/decision), and that right ventricular failure is the leading cause of early mortality."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-lvad-b08-03","section":"lvad","category":"mcs","topic":"Pimping ceiling — trials, device generations and the transplant question","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — trials, device generations and the transplant question","text":"Understand the relationship to {{c1::heart transplantation}}: transplantation remains the definitive therapy with the best long-term survival but is limited by donor scarcity, so LVADs bridge suitable candidates and provide durable destination therapy for those who are not candidates; some patients need biventricular support (a right-sided device, RVAD, in addition, i.e."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b01-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — why we repair a degenerative mitral valve instead of replacing it","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — why we repair a degenerative mitral valve instead of replacing it","text":"The central teaching point of this entire article is that for {{c1::degenerative MR}}, repair is preferred over replacement whenever it is technically feasible, and this preference is not a stylistic one — it is backed by outcomes."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b01-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — why we repair a degenerative mitral valve instead of replacing it","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — why we repair a degenerative mitral valve instead of replacing it","text":"The {{c1::mitral valve}} is the inflow valve of the left ventricle."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b01-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — why we repair a degenerative mitral valve instead of replacing it","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — why we repair a degenerative mitral valve instead of replacing it","text":"It has to do two contradictory things well: open wide enough in diastole to admit the entire stroke volume from the {{c1::left atrium}} in a fraction of a second, and then seal completely in systole against the highest pressure the heart generates, all while being tethered from below by a subvalvular apparatus that must relax and tense in perfect coordination with the ventricle around it."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b02-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Mitral anatomy — the saddle, the segments, and what runs nearby","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Mitral anatomy — the saddle, the segments, and what runs nearby","text":"The mitral annulus is not a flat ring — it is a three-dimensional {{c1::saddle shape}}, higher anteriorly and posteriorly and lower at the two commissures, and this shape reduces leaflet and chordal stress across the cardiac cycle."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b02-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Mitral anatomy — the saddle, the segments, and what runs nearby","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Mitral anatomy — the saddle, the segments, and what runs nearby","text":"The valve has two leaflets of very different shape: the {{c1::anterior leaflet}} is tall, roughly semicircular, and occupies about a third of the annular circumference but around half the valve orifice area, while the posterior leaflet is shorter but occupies about two-thirds of the annular circumference and is naturally scalloped into three segments."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b02-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Mitral anatomy — the saddle, the segments, and what runs nearby","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Mitral anatomy — the saddle, the segments, and what runs nearby","text":"By convention (the Carpentier nomenclature) the posterior leaflet segments are labelled {{c1::P1 (lateral/anterolateral), P2 (middle), and P3 (medial/posteromedial)}}, and the anterior leaflet is divided into corresponding, though not truly scalloped, regions A1, A2 and A3 directly opposite them."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b03-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"The Carpentier functional classification and the degenerative spectrum","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"The Carpentier functional classification and the degenerative spectrum","text":"The {{c1::Carpentier classification}} describes leaflet motion, not the underlying cause: Type I is normal leaflet motion with a leak from annular dilation or a leaflet perforation (as in endocarditis); Type II is excessive leaflet motion — prolapse, where part of the leaflet edge rises above the annular plane in systole, the hallmark of degenerative disease; Type IIIa is restricted leaflet motion in both diastole and systole, typically from rheumatic thickening and chordal fusion; and Type IIIb is restricted motion specifically in systole, the pattern of ischemic or functional MR where a dilated, tethering ventricle pulls the leaflets down and apart."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b03-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"The Carpentier functional classification and the degenerative spectrum","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"The Carpentier functional classification and the degenerative spectrum","text":"Degenerative MR is therefore a {{c1::Type II}} lesion, and everything in this article about resection, neochordae and annuloplasty is aimed at correcting excess leaflet motion while preserving or restoring normal coaptation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b03-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"The Carpentier functional classification and the degenerative spectrum","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"The Carpentier functional classification and the degenerative spectrum","text":"Carpentier in one line — {{c1::I normal, II too much, IIIa stuck shut (diastole, rheumatic), IIIb stuck}} open (systole, ischemic/functional)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b04-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup, and timing of surgery for severe primary MR","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup, and timing of surgery for severe primary MR","text":"Diagnosis and severity grading rest on {{c1::transthoracic echocardiography}} (with transesophageal imaging used intraoperatively and whenever transthoracic images are inadequate), assessing regurgitant volume and fraction, effective regurgitant orifice area, vena contracta width, pulmonary vein flow reversal, and left atrial and ventricular size, together with the mechanism (which leaflet segment prolapses, and why)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b04-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup, and timing of surgery for severe primary MR","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup, and timing of surgery for severe primary MR","text":"The 2020 ACC/AHA valve guideline indications for intervention in {{c1::severe primary (degenerative) MR}} are built around this logic."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b04-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup, and timing of surgery for severe primary MR","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup, and timing of surgery for severe primary MR","text":"Surgery is indicated for symptomatic {{c1::severe primary MR}} regardless of LV function (assuming the patient is an operative candidate)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b05-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Repair techniques — the toolkit for restoring coaptation","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Repair techniques — the toolkit for restoring coaptation","text":"For the leaflet, the classic technique for a focal posterior leaflet prolapse (commonly P2) is {{c1::triangular or quadrangular resection}} — the redundant, prolapsing segment is excised as a wedge or rectangle and the remaining edges are sewn back together, restoring a normal height and eliminating the excess tissue that was billowing above the coaptation line."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b05-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Repair techniques — the toolkit for restoring coaptation","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Repair techniques — the toolkit for restoring coaptation","text":"When a wider segment is resected, a {{c1::sliding leaflet plasty}} detaches the leaflet from the annulus on either side of the resection, slides the remaining leaflet tissue together, and reattaches it to the annulus, reducing overall leaflet height so the repaired segment does not stand taller than its neighbors and produce a new, secondary prolapse."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b05-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Repair techniques — the toolkit for restoring coaptation","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Repair techniques — the toolkit for restoring coaptation","text":"An increasingly dominant alternative, especially useful for anterior leaflet prolapse (where resection risks creating outflow tract obstruction or an unstable leaflet) or when native chordae are simply too few or too degenerated to preserve, is {{c1::artificial neochordae}} — loops of expanded PTFE (Gore-Tex) suture placed from the papillary muscle head to the free edge of the prolapsing segment, replacing the function of ruptured or elongated native chordae without removing any leaflet tissue at all."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b06-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The single most important quality-control step of the entire operation happens after weaning: {{c1::intraoperative transesophageal echocardiography}} is used to confirm the repair is competent (no more than trivial residual MR), that there is no systolic anterior motion of the anterior leaflet causing dynamic outflow obstruction, and that the mitral valve area is not iatrogenically stenotic — if any of these is unsatisfactory, the surgeon returns to bypass and revises the repair (or, rarely, converts to replacement) before leaving the operating room, because a mitral repair is not considered complete until it has passed this on-table echo test."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b06-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"{{c1::Mitral repair}} is performed on cardiopulmonary bypass with a still, decompressed, open heart, whether approached through a median sternotomy or a minimally invasive right thoracotomy/port approach; the exposure differs but the intracardiac steps are the same."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b06-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"{{c1::Bicaval venous cannulation}} with caval snares is used (not a single two-stage cannula), because the left atrium must be opened and kept dry of all venous return during the repair, and the aorta is cross-clamped with cardioplegic arrest as in any open-chamber procedure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b07-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Systolic anterior motion (SAM) — mechanism and management","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Systolic anterior motion (SAM) — mechanism and management","text":"The first-line management is therefore entirely medical and physiologic, addressed in a specific order: give {{c1::volume}} to fill and enlarge the ventricle, increase afterload with a vasoconstrictor (raising resistance widens the outflow tract and slows the Venturi-accelerated flow that drags the leaflet into it), and reduce or stop inotropes, since a hypercontractile ventricle worsens the obstruction."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b07-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Systolic anterior motion (SAM) — mechanism and management","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Systolic anterior motion (SAM) — mechanism and management","text":"Systolic anterior motion of the {{c1::mitral valve}} is a dynamic complication in which the anterior leaflet (or the repaired posterior leaflet edge, pulled by chordae) is drawn anteriorly into the left ventricular outflow tract during systole, obstructing outflow and typically dragging the mitral valve open enough to produce a new, often severe, posteriorly directed regurgitant jet."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b07-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Systolic anterior motion (SAM) — mechanism and management","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Systolic anterior motion (SAM) — mechanism and management","text":"It happens because a repair — most often one using a {{c1::large ring}} that reduces the annulus aggressively, combined with a posterior leaflet that is left too tall — leaves surplus leaflet coaptation tissue positioned close to the outflow tract, and this redundant leaflet is then caught by the high-velocity systolic flow stream (the Venturi effect draws it into the outflow path) in a ventricle that is small, hypercontractile, or under low afterload, all of which narrow the outflow tract or accelerate flow through it."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b08-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and their management","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications and their management","text":"A {{c1::technically successful repair}} is judged by two numbers on post-repair echo, not one: no more than trivial regurgitation AND a non-stenotic diastolic valve area."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b08-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and their management","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications and their management","text":"Chasing a perfect systolic seal by over-resecting or over-reducing the {{c1::annulus}} can silently trade regurgitation for stenosis."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b08-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and their management","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications and their management","text":"A technically successful repair is judged by two numbers on {{c1::post-repair echo}}, not one: no more than trivial regurgitation AND a non-stenotic diastolic valve area."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b09-01","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classification, thresholds and the repair-versus-replace argument","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classification, thresholds and the repair-versus-replace argument","text":"Own the {{c1::Carpentier classification}} cold: Type I (normal motion, annular dilation or perforation), Type II (excess motion/prolapse — degenerative MR), Type IIIa (restricted in diastole — rheumatic), Type IIIb (restricted in systole — ischemic/functional)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b09-02","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classification, thresholds and the repair-versus-replace argument","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classification, thresholds and the repair-versus-replace argument","text":"Know the degenerative spectrum by name: {{c1::fibroelastic deficiency}} (focal, usually single chordal rupture, often P2, near-normal surrounding tissue) versus Barlow disease (diffuse myxomatous excess, multiple segments, annular dilation, technically harder to repair)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mitral-repair-b09-03","section":"mitral-repair","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classification, thresholds and the repair-versus-replace argument","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classification, thresholds and the repair-versus-replace argument","text":"Know the mitral segment nomenclature ({{c1::A1/A2/A3}} anterior opposite P1/P2/P3 posterior) and that the annuloplasty ring is sized to the anterior leaflet, because it is the stable reference dimension in a valve where the posterior annulus is what dilates."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b01-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — the mitral apparatus you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the mitral apparatus you forgot","text":"This single fact drives the central technical principle of modern mitral replacement: preserve the {{c1::subvalvular apparatus}} wherever possible."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b01-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — the mitral apparatus you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the mitral apparatus you forgot","text":"The {{c1::circumflex artery}} and the coronary sinus run in the left atrioventricular groove immediately behind the posterior mitral annulus."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b01-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — the mitral apparatus you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the mitral apparatus you forgot","text":"The {{c1::mitral valve}} is not just two leaflets; it is a coordinated apparatus of five parts working as one unit, and understanding all five is the difference between a replacement that helps and one that ruptures the heart."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b02-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why the apparatus governs the ventricle","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why the apparatus governs the ventricle","text":"Two failure modes bring a patient to the operating room. {{c1::Mitral stenosis}} is a valve that will not open — classically from rheumatic disease that fuses the commissures and thickens and calcifies the leaflets and chordae, obstructing filling, raising left atrial and pulmonary pressures, and producing atrial fibrillation and pulmonary congestion. Mitral regurgitation is a valve that will not seal — the ventricle wastes work pumping blood backward into the atrium, which dilates, and the volume-overloaded ventricle eventually fails."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b02-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why the apparatus governs the ventricle","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why the apparatus governs the ventricle","text":"Consider what the {{c1::mitral valve}} does across the cardiac cycle."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b02-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why the apparatus governs the ventricle","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — why the apparatus governs the ventricle","text":"In diastole it opens widely so the {{c1::left atrium}} can fill the left ventricle at low pressure; in systole it must seal completely so that when the ventricle contracts, all the stroke volume is ejected forward through the aortic valve and none leaks backward."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b03-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup and the decision to replace rather than repair","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and the decision to replace rather than repair","text":"The classic indications for replacement rather than repair are a {{c1::rheumatic valve}} with fused, rigid, calcified leaflets and thickened chordae that cannot be made both competent and mobile; extensive mitral annular calcification; active infective endocarditis with destruction of the leaflets and annulus; a failed prior repair; and many cases of ischaemic or functional (secondary) mitral regurgitation, where the valve leaflets are structurally normal but tethered by a dilated, remodelled ventricle and where the evidence for durable benefit from repair is weaker."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b03-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup and the decision to replace rather than repair","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and the decision to replace rather than repair","text":"{{c1::Repair}} is the preferred operation for most primary degenerative mitral regurgitation because it preserves the native apparatus, avoids a prosthesis and its lifelong risks, and delivers better durability and survival."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b03-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup and the decision to replace rather than repair","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and the decision to replace rather than repair","text":"{{c1::Replacement}} is chosen when a durable repair is not achievable or not appropriate."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b04-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The patient is placed on cardiopulmonary bypass with {{c1::bicaval cannulation}} and caval snares, because the left atrium and the mitral valve are approached by opening the heart and the field must be dry."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b04-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"Rather than excising everything, the surgeon {{c1::preserves the posterior leaflet and its chordae}} intact against the annulus, and where the anterior leaflet must be removed for exposure or because it is diseased, its chordae may be detached with a button of leaflet and reattached to the annulus to keep annulo-papillary continuity."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b04-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The sutures are passed through the sewing ring of the correctly sized prosthesis, which is then {{c1::parachuted down}} onto the annulus and the sutures tied, orienting a mechanical valve's struts or a bioprosthesis's posts away from the left ventricular outflow tract."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b05-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Subvalvular preservation and the left ventricular rupture it prevents","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Subvalvular preservation and the left ventricular rupture it prevents","text":"The most feared technical catastrophe of mitral replacement is {{c1::left ventricular rupture}}, a tear in the ventricular wall that produces sudden, massive, often fatal bleeding, typically after separation from bypass when the ventricle is filled and pressurized."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b05-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Subvalvular preservation and the left ventricular rupture it prevents","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Subvalvular preservation and the left ventricular rupture it prevents","text":"It is classically described in three types by location: {{c1::Type I}} is a tear at the atrioventricular groove (the junction of the atrium, annulus and ventricle); Type II is a tear at the base of a papillary muscle, produced when a papillary muscle is over-vigorously excised or avulsed; and Type III is a subannular midventricular tear between those two levels."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b05-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Subvalvular preservation and the left ventricular rupture it prevents","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Subvalvular preservation and the left ventricular rupture it prevents","text":"Sudden, torrential dark bleeding from behind the heart after weaning from bypass following mitral replacement is {{c1::left ventricular rupture}} until proven otherwise."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b06-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical versus bioprosthetic and anticoagulation","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical versus bioprosthetic and anticoagulation","text":"A {{c1::mechanical valve}} (a bileaflet pyrolytic-carbon design) is extremely durable and essentially never wears out, but it is thrombogenic and mandates lifelong warfarin anticoagulation; at the mitral position the target international normalized ratio is 2.5 to 3.5, higher than the aortic target because the lower-pressure, lower-velocity flow across a mitral prosthesis is more prone to thrombosis."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b06-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical versus bioprosthetic and anticoagulation","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical versus bioprosthetic and anticoagulation","text":"A {{c1::bioprosthetic valve}} (bovine pericardial or porcine tissue) avoids the need for lifelong anticoagulation (usually a short period of anticoagulation or antiplatelet therapy after implantation, then aspirin) but undergoes structural valve deterioration over years, faster in younger patients, and eventually needs reintervention."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b06-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical versus bioprosthetic and anticoagulation","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical versus bioprosthetic and anticoagulation","text":"Direct oral anticoagulants are not approved for mechanical valves; {{c1::warfarin}} is required."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b07-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"The management principle across these is that a new gradient, a new murmur, heart failure, haemolysis, fever, or embolism after a {{c1::mitral prosthesis}} is a diagnostic problem demanding echocardiography (transoesophageal for the mitral prosthesis), not reassurance."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b07-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"{{c1::Anticoagulation}} must be meticulous for mechanical valves, and any suspicion of thrombosis or endocarditis is investigated promptly."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b07-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"The management principle across these is that a new gradient, a new murmur, heart failure, haemolysis, fever, or embolism after a mitral {{c1::prosthesis}} is a diagnostic problem demanding echocardiography (transoesophageal for the mitral prosthesis), not reassurance."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b08-01","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, prosthesis-patient mismatch and transcatheter options","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, prosthesis-patient mismatch and transcatheter options","text":"The {{c1::mechanical mitral INR target}} is 2.5 to 3.5; the aortic mechanical target is lower (around 2.0 to 3.0 or 2.5) because mitral prostheses sit in lower-velocity flow and thrombose more readily."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b08-02","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, prosthesis-patient mismatch and transcatheter options","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, prosthesis-patient mismatch and transcatheter options","text":"Know the {{c1::three types of left ventricular rupture}} (I atrioventricular groove, II papillary muscle base, III subannular) and that subvalvular preservation prevents Types I and II."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-mvr-b08-03","section":"mvr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, prosthesis-patient mismatch and transcatheter options","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, prosthesis-patient mismatch and transcatheter options","text":"{{c1::Prosthesis-patient mismatch}} applies at the mitral position too: an indexed effective orifice area that is too small for the patient leaves a residual transmitral gradient; the mitral indexed-EOA thresholds are lower than the aortic (moderate mismatch around 1.2 cm squared per m squared, severe around 0.9)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b01-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Start here — what changes when you take the pump away","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what changes when you take the pump away","text":"In OPCAB the heart keeps beating and keeps perfusing the body throughout the operation; instead of arresting the whole heart, the surgeon {{c1::mechanically stabilizes just the small patch of moving muscle}} around each target coronary artery long enough to sew an anastomosis, while the rest of the heart continues to contract and eject normally."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b01-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Start here — what changes when you take the pump away","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what changes when you take the pump away","text":"Understanding OPCAB starts from what it deliberately avoids, because every design feature of the operation traces back to one of two things: avoiding {{c1::cardiopulmonary bypass}} itself, and avoiding aortic manipulation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b01-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Start here — what changes when you take the pump away","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — what changes when you take the pump away","text":"On-pump CABG, covered in its own article, arrests the heart and borrows the {{c1::circulation}} with a machine so the surgeon can work on a still, bloodless field."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b02-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — the two problems OPCAB has to solve","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — the two problems OPCAB has to solve","text":"This is solved with a {{c1::mechanical stabilizer}} — most commonly a suction-based epicardial device (the Octopus platform is the best-known example) that applies two parallel suction feet straddling the target vessel and gently compresses/tethers that local segment against the underlying myocardium, damping local wall motion to near stillness while the surrounding heart continues to beat and eject."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b02-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — the two problems OPCAB has to solve","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — the two problems OPCAB has to solve","text":"A second technology, an apical suction cup (the {{c1::Starfish}} device is the prototype), grips the cardiac apex and allows the surgeon to lift, rotate and reposition the whole heart out of its normal orientation — verticalize it — to expose posterior and lateral wall targets (circumflex-territory vessels, in particular) that are otherwise buried against the pericardium and diaphragm."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b02-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — the two problems OPCAB has to solve","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — the two problems OPCAB has to solve","text":"The {{c1::right ventricle}} tolerates this positioning far worse than the left, since it is thin-walled and highly preload-dependent; a heart that is verticalized too aggressively, or in a patient with borderline RV function to begin with, can suddenly lose forward flow."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b03-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Enabling technology and technique — the tools that make a beating anastomosis possible","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Enabling technology and technique — the tools that make a beating anastomosis possible","text":"Beyond the epicardial (Octopus-type) stabilizer and the apical (Starfish-type) positioner, several other tools are routinely used together during an off-pump anastomosis. {{c1::Intracoronary shunts}} — a small soft plastic tube inserted into the open, incised coronary artery during the anastomosis — keep a channel of blood flowing through the target vessel to the myocardium distal to the arteriotomy while the suture line is placed around the shunt, protecting that territory from the ischaemia that would otherwise occur for the several minutes the vessel is open and not yet grafted."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b03-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Enabling technology and technique — the tools that make a beating anastomosis possible","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Enabling technology and technique — the tools that make a beating anastomosis possible","text":"A {{c1::blower-mister}} is a fine jet of humidified carbon dioxide (or saline mist) directed gently across the operative field during the anastomosis, clearing blood from the field so the surgeon can actually see the arteriotomy and suture line without a bloodless, cross-clamped, cardioplegia-arrested field to work in — this single tool is one of the more understated enablers of OPCAB, since visualizing a beating, actively perfused coronary artery is otherwise very difficult."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b03-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Enabling technology and technique — the tools that make a beating anastomosis possible","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Enabling technology and technique — the tools that make a beating anastomosis possible","text":"{{c1::Deep pericardial retraction (traction) sutures}}, placed at specific points on the posterior pericardium (classically near the left inferior pulmonary vein and the pericardial reflection near the inferior vena cava), pull the heart up and rotate it into the plane needed to expose lateral and inferior targets, working in combination with the apical positioner rather than instead of it."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b04-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Anticoagulation, monitoring, and the standing readiness to convert","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation, monitoring, and the standing readiness to convert","text":"The heparin dose used for OPCAB is {{c1::lower than the full CPB dose}}, and the target activated clotting time (ACT) is correspondingly lower — generally kept above about 300 seconds rather than the 400 to 480 second target used for full bypass, reflecting that there is no foreign circuit tubing and oxygenator membrane to protect against clotting, only the open coronary artery and the fresh anastomoses."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b04-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Anticoagulation, monitoring, and the standing readiness to convert","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation, monitoring, and the standing readiness to convert","text":"Even without {{c1::cardiopulmonary bypass}}, OPCAB requires systemic anticoagulation, because the coronary artery is opened and a foreign conduit is sewn into the arterial circulation, both of which are thrombogenic."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b04-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Anticoagulation, monitoring, and the standing readiness to convert","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Anticoagulation, monitoring, and the standing readiness to convert","text":"Because OPCAB deliberately keeps native {{c1::circulation}} and native pump function running, the operating team must be able to convert to full cardiopulmonary bypass at any moment if the heart cannot tolerate positioning, if haemodynamics deteriorate despite volume and repositioning, or if a technical problem (bleeding, an unstable anastomosis, ischaemia from a shunt malfunction) demands a stable, motionless field to fix."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b05-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"The anaortic (no-touch aorta) technique","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The anaortic (no-touch aorta) technique","text":"The most complete expression of OPCAB's aortic-avoidance philosophy is the {{c1::anaortic (\"no-touch aorta\") technique}}, in which every graft is constructed without placing any clamp on the ascending aorta at all."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b05-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"The anaortic (no-touch aorta) technique","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The anaortic (no-touch aorta) technique","text":"This matters most for a specific patient: one with a heavily calcified, non-clampable ascending aorta — in its most severe form a {{c1::porcelain aorta}}, an aorta with circumferential calcification that cannot be safely cannulated or clamped at all — or any patient otherwise identified as being at high embolic stroke risk (prior stroke, extensive atheroma on imaging or intraoperative epiaortic ultrasound, advanced age with diffuse vascular disease)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b05-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"The anaortic (no-touch aorta) technique","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The anaortic (no-touch aorta) technique","text":"This is achieved by {{c1::using only in-situ arterial inflow}} — the LIMA and, when a second arterial conduit is needed, the right internal thoracic artery or a radial artery configured as a composite (Y or T) graft off the LIMA rather than as an independent graft with its own proximal anastomosis on the aorta — so that every distal target is reached without a single aortic clamp application for either cannulation or a proximal anastomosis."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b06-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"The randomized evidence — ROOBY, CORONARY and GOPCABE","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The randomized evidence — ROOBY, CORONARY and GOPCABE","text":"The trial trio — \"{{c1::ROOBY warns about the learner, CORONARY reassures the expert, GOPCABE reassures}} for the elderly.\" Every one of these trials converges on the same real message: off-pump outcomes are surgeon- and centre-experience-dependent, not a fixed property of the technique itself."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b06-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"The randomized evidence — ROOBY, CORONARY and GOPCABE","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The randomized evidence — ROOBY, CORONARY and GOPCABE","text":"The trial trio — \"{{c1::ROOBY}} warns about the learner, CORONARY reassures the expert, GOPCABE reassures for the elderly.\" Every one of these trials converges on the same real message: off-pump outcomes are surgeon- and centre-experience-dependent, not a fixed property of the technique itself."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b06-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"The randomized evidence — ROOBY, CORONARY and GOPCABE","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The randomized evidence — ROOBY, CORONARY and GOPCABE","text":"Three trials define what a senior trainee is expected to know about off-pump versus on-pump outcomes, and they do not all agree, which is itself the teaching point. {{c1::ROOBY}}, a US Veterans Affairs trial, compared off-pump to on-pump CABG and found worse composite outcomes and worse graft patency with off-pump surgery; its most important limitation, widely discussed, is that many of its participating surgeons had relatively limited off-pump experience, so the trial is often read less as \"off-pump is inferior\" and more as \"off-pump outcomes are highly dependent on surgeon and centre experience,\" a conclusion reinforced by essentially every other line of evidence in this space."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b07-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Who benefits most, and the tradeoffs against on-pump grafting","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Who benefits most, and the tradeoffs against on-pump grafting","text":"Put together, the consistent signal across the trial evidence and observational data is that off-pump surgery {{c1::lowers transfusion requirements, perioperative stroke, and acute kidney injury}} compared with on-pump surgery, benefits that trace directly back to avoiding the CPB inflammatory response, avoiding haemodilution, and, for stroke specifically, avoiding aortic manipulation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b07-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Who benefits most, and the tradeoffs against on-pump grafting","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Who benefits most, and the tradeoffs against on-pump grafting","text":"Against this, off-pump surgery carries a real risk of {{c1::incomplete revascularization}} — because working around a beating, positioned heart is technically harder, especially for posterior and lateral targets, some surgeons in some cases graft fewer vessels than the angiogram would ideally call for — and a signal in less experienced hands (as in ROOBY) toward lower graft patency, presumably reflecting the greater technical difficulty of a precise anastomosis on a moving, positioned target compared with a still, arrested one."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b07-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Who benefits most, and the tradeoffs against on-pump grafting","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Who benefits most, and the tradeoffs against on-pump grafting","text":"The patients who benefit most from a deliberate off-pump strategy are exactly the ones for whom the two things OPCAB avoids are most dangerous: a patient with a {{c1::porcelain or heavily atheromatous, high-stroke-risk aorta}} who cannot safely be cannulated or clamped, and a patient whose comorbidities make the CPB inflammatory response and haemodilution particularly hazardous — for example significant chronic kidney disease (where avoiding haemodilution and the pump-related renal insult lowers AKI risk), frailty, or advanced age."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b08-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"Persisting with an off-pump strategy through worsening {{c1::haemodynamic instability}}, in the hope that the last one or two grafts can still be completed, converts a controlled, elective decision into an emergent, higher-risk one."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b08-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"The {{c1::safer path}} is early, deliberate conversion to on-pump support the moment positioning-related instability fails to respond promptly to standard countermeasures."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b08-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"Persisting with an off-pump strategy through {{c1::worsening haemodynamic instability}}, in the hope that the last one or two grafts can still be completed, converts a controlled, elective decision into an emergent, higher-risk one."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b09-01","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, named trials and the fine print","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, named trials and the fine print","text":"Know the anticoagulation numbers cold: OPCAB uses a {{c1::lower heparin dose}} than full CPB, with an ACT target generally kept above about 300 seconds, compared with the 400 to 480 second target for full bypass — and know why the lower target is acceptable (no extracorporeal circuit surface to protect)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b09-02","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, named trials and the fine print","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, named trials and the fine print","text":"Know the stabilizer and positioner technology by name — the {{c1::Octopus}} epicardial suction stabilizer damps local wall motion at the target vessel, and the Starfish apical suction device verticalizes and repositions the whole heart — plus intracoronary shunts (protect distal myocardial flow during the open anastomosis) and the blower-mister (clears the field for visualization)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-opcab-b09-03","section":"opcab","category":"coronary","topic":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, named trials and the fine print","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — numbers, named trials and the fine print","text":"Know the trial trio and their nuance: {{c1::ROOBY}} showed worse composite outcomes and graft patency off-pump, but is best interpreted through the lens of limited surgeon experience in that particular cohort rather than as a condemnation of the technique itself; CORONARY, run with experienced off-pump surgeons, showed no significant difference in death or MI at 30 days or one year, with reduced transfusion, AKI and reoperation for bleeding off-pump; GOPCABE extended a similar no-difference finding on the primary composite to patients 75 and older, with reduced transfusion and respiratory failure off-pump."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b01-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — why the aortic valve fails and why we replace it","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — why the aortic valve fails and why we replace it","text":"The {{c1::aortic valve}} is a one-way door between the left ventricle and the aorta."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b01-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — why the aortic valve fails and why we replace it","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — why the aortic valve fails and why we replace it","text":"It opens in systole so the {{c1::ventricle}} can eject its stroke volume against almost no resistance, and it closes in diastole so that blood already ejected into the aorta cannot fall back into the ventricle."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b01-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — why the aortic valve fails and why we replace it","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — why the aortic valve fails and why we replace it","text":"It has {{c1::no muscle}} of its own and no active mechanism — it is a purely passive structure made of three thin, pliable cusps that billow open with forward flow and coapt shut the instant pressure reverses."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b02-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"The aortic root — anatomy you must know cold before you touch it","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"The aortic root — anatomy you must know cold before you touch it","text":"At its base is the {{c1::annulus}}, the crown-shaped (not planar) attachment ring of the three cusps, which is also the sewing ring for a prosthesis."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b02-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"The aortic root — anatomy you must know cold before you touch it","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"The aortic root — anatomy you must know cold before you touch it","text":"Above the annulus the root bulges outward into three pouches called the {{c1::sinuses of Valsalva}} — the right, left and noncoronary sinuses — named for which cusp sits opposite them, and they exist so that during systole the cusps do not plaster themselves against the aortic wall and occlude the coronary ostia."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b02-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"The aortic root — anatomy you must know cold before you touch it","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"The aortic root — anatomy you must know cold before you touch it","text":"The {{c1::right coronary artery}} arises from the right sinus and the left main coronary artery from the left sinus; the noncoronary sinus gives rise to no coronary artery."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b03-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — how the ventricle adapts, and how it decompensates","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — how the ventricle adapts, and how it decompensates","text":"The three classic symptoms of severe AS — {{c1::angina, syncope and heart failure}} — track this physiology directly: angina from the supply-demand mismatch described above, syncope from an inability to augment cardiac output during exertional vasodilation (a fixed-output state with peripheral vasodilation drops cerebral perfusion), and heart failure once the ventricle finally decompensates."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b03-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — how the ventricle adapts, and how it decompensates","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — how the ventricle adapts, and how it decompensates","text":"Aortic stenosis imposes a fixed, progressively worsening resistance on left ventricular ejection, and the ventricle's initial response is compensatory concentric hypertrophy — the wall thickens so that wall stress (which by Laplace's law rises with {{c1::pressure}} and chamber radius but falls as wall thickness increases) stays roughly normal despite the high intracavitary pressure needed to cross the valve."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b03-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — how the ventricle adapts, and how it decompensates","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — how the ventricle adapts, and how it decompensates","text":"For years this compensation is {{c1::remarkably effective}}: ejection fraction is preserved, cardiac output at rest is normal, and the patient is asymptomatic."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b04-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup, echo thresholds and indications for surgery","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup, echo thresholds and indications for surgery","text":"The diagnostic and severity-defining test is {{c1::transthoracic echocardiography}}, which measures three linked parameters across the valve: the peak jet velocity (Vmax), the mean transvalvular gradient, and the aortic valve area (AVA) calculated by the continuity equation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b04-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup, echo thresholds and indications for surgery","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup, echo thresholds and indications for surgery","text":"Parameter Severe AS Very severe AS Peak jet velocity (Vmax) >=4.0 m/s >=5.0 m/s Mean gradient >=40 mmHg >=60 mmHg Aortic valve area (AVA) <=1.0 cm2 very small Indexed AVA <=0.6 cm2/m2 - A frequent trap is the patient whose ventricle cannot generate enough forward flow to produce a high gradient even though the valve is anatomically severe — {{c1::low-flow, low-gradient AS}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b04-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup, echo thresholds and indications for surgery","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup, echo thresholds and indications for surgery","text":"In the classic (reduced-EF) form, EF is under 50%, the mean gradient is under 40, and stroke volume index is under 35 mL/m2; here {{c1::dobutamine stress echocardiography}} is used to distinguish truly severe AS from pseudosevere AS by giving the ventricle contractile reserve to work with — if AVA stays at or below 1.0 cm2 as flow rises, the stenosis is truly severe (fixed anatomic obstruction); if AVA rises above 1.0 cm2 with augmented flow, the valve was never severely stenotic and the low gradient simply reflected low flow (pseudosevere)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b05-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Aortic regurgitation — the other lesion, and why it behaves nothing like stenosis","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Aortic regurgitation — the other lesion, and why it behaves nothing like stenosis","text":"The classification you will be asked for by name is the {{c1::El Khoury functional classification}}, which is built on cusp motion the way Carpentier's is for the mitral valve: Type I is normal cusp motion with regurgitation from dilation of the root components or from a cusp perforation, Type II is cusp prolapse, and Type III is cusp restriction."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b05-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Aortic regurgitation — the other lesion, and why it behaves nothing like stenosis","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Aortic regurgitation — the other lesion, and why it behaves nothing like stenosis","text":"In chronic AR the ventricle faces a combined {{c1::volume and pressure load}} — every beat it must eject the forward stroke volume plus the volume that leaked back in diastole — and it answers with eccentric hypertrophy, so that a very large total stroke volume is delivered at a nearly normal filling pressure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b05-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Aortic regurgitation — the other lesion, and why it behaves nothing like stenosis","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Aortic regurgitation — the other lesion, and why it behaves nothing like stenosis","text":"The compensatory tachycardia in acute severe AR is {{c1::protective}} — it shortens diastole and therefore shortens regurgitation time — so blunting it with beta blockade is harmful, and an intra-aortic balloon pump is absolutely contraindicated because diastolic augmentation drives more blood backwards across the incompetent valve."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b06-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical, bioprosthetic, and the small annulus","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical, bioprosthetic, and the small annulus","text":"A {{c1::mechanical valve}} — most commonly a bileaflet tilting-disc design — is essentially indestructible and will likely outlast the patient, but it is thrombogenic and requires lifelong warfarin anticoagulation (target INR around 2.5, roughly 2.0 to 3.0, for an aortic mechanical valve with no other risk factors), with the attendant lifelong bleeding risk and the practical burden of monitoring."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b06-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical, bioprosthetic, and the small annulus","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical, bioprosthetic, and the small annulus","text":"A {{c1::bioprosthetic valve}} — a tissue valve, typically bovine pericardial or porcine, mounted on a stent frame or, for the newer sutureless/rapid-deployment designs, a compressible frame — requires no long-term anticoagulation beyond a few months of low-dose aspirin or, in some protocols, a brief period of anticoagulation, but it degenerates over time (structural valve deterioration) and a younger recipient is more likely to outlive it and need a reoperation or a transcatheter valve-in-valve procedure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b06-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical, bioprosthetic, and the small annulus","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Choosing the prosthesis — mechanical, bioprosthetic, and the small annulus","text":"As a working framework: mechanical is favored {{c1::under about age 50}}, where durability without reoperation matters most and a long anticoagulated life is anticipated anyway; bioprosthetic is favored above about age 65 to 70, where the durability of a bioprosthesis usually exceeds remaining life expectancy and avoiding anticoagulation in an older patient (falls risk, other comorbidities) matters more; and the 50-to-70 range is a genuine shared decision between patient values (lifestyle, anticoagulation tolerance, pregnancy plans, bleeding risk, likelihood of needing another cardiac procedure) and the surgical team."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b07-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Aortic root enlargement — when the annulus is too small for the valve the patient needs","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Aortic root enlargement — when the annulus is too small for the valve the patient needs","text":"Divide the effective orifice area by body surface area and you get the {{c1::indexed EOA}}, the number that defines patient-prosthesis mismatch: moderate PPM at EOAi below 0.85 cm2/m2 and severe PPM at EOAi below 0.65 cm2/m2."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b07-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Aortic root enlargement — when the annulus is too small for the valve the patient needs","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Aortic root enlargement — when the annulus is too small for the valve the patient needs","text":"The essential discipline is that PPM is {{c1::predicted, not discovered}} — every manufacturer publishes reference EOA values for each model and size, so once your sizer tells you a 21 mm valve is the largest that will seat, you divide that valve's published EOA by the patient's BSA before you commit."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b07-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Aortic root enlargement — when the annulus is too small for the valve the patient needs","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Aortic root enlargement — when the annulus is too small for the valve the patient needs","text":"The named enlargement procedures sort cleanly into {{c1::posterior and anterior}} techniques, and you should be able to draw each one and say exactly which sinus the incision runs through and what tissue it ends in."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b08-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"A transverse (oblique) {{c1::aortotomy}} is made in the ascending aorta above the sinotubular junction and carried around most of the circumference, exposing the diseased valve from above."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b08-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The three native cusps are then sharply excised close to the annulus, and the surgeon meticulously {{c1::debrides calcium}} from the annular tissue — this step is where excessive depth or force risks tearing into the aortomitral curtain posteriorly (annular rupture) or compressing the conduction axis at the noncoronary-right commissure, so it is done under direct vision with small, controlled bites."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b08-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"The annulus is then sized with valve sizers to select the largest prosthesis that fits the anatomy without oversizing, and {{c1::pledgeted mattress sutures}} (or, in some techniques, a running suture) are placed circumferentially around the annulus, then passed through the sewing cuff of the prosthesis, which is lowered (\"parachuted\") down the sutures into position and the sutures are tied."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b09-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Variations — bicuspid disease, minimally invasive access, and SAVR versus TAVI","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Variations — bicuspid disease, minimally invasive access, and SAVR versus TAVI","text":"A meaningful minority of patients presenting with severe AS, especially younger ones, have a congenitally {{c1::bicuspid aortic valve}} rather than a normal trileaflet valve that has calcified."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b09-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Variations — bicuspid disease, minimally invasive access, and SAVR versus TAVI","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Variations — bicuspid disease, minimally invasive access, and SAVR versus TAVI","text":"The {{c1::Sievers classification}} describes the morphology by the number of raphes (a raphe is a fibrous ridge marking a fused commissure between two cusps that never fully separated in development): type 0 has no raphe (a true two-cusp valve), type 1 has one raphe (the most common pattern, usually a fusion between the right and left coronary cusps), and type 2 has two raphes."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b09-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Variations — bicuspid disease, minimally invasive access, and SAVR versus TAVI","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Variations — bicuspid disease, minimally invasive access, and SAVR versus TAVI","text":"Bicuspid valves are clinically important beyond the valve itself because they are strongly associated with an {{c1::aortopathy}} — dilation and an increased risk of aneurysm and dissection of the ascending aorta — driven by an intrinsic connective-tissue abnormality in the aortic wall rather than purely hemodynamic stress from the abnormal valve."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b10-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and their management","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Complications and their management","text":"Less specific to SAVR but still important: {{c1::prosthetic valve endocarditis}}, postoperative atrial fibrillation (extremely common after any open cardiac procedure and usually self-limited with rate/rhythm control and anticoagulation as indicated), and bleeding related to anticoagulation in mechanical-valve recipients."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b10-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and their management","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Complications and their management","text":"Sudden ST-segment elevation and {{c1::hemodynamic collapse}} as the patient is weaned from bypass after a fresh AVR is coronary ostial obstruction until proven otherwise — this is a surgical emergency requiring immediate recognition, not a slow workup."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b10-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and their management","partId":"block:10","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":9,"sourceTitle":"Complications and their management","text":"When the case included a root enlargement, {{c1::bleeding at the triple point}} where patch, native annulus and sewing cuff meet is the commonest patch-specific complication, and it is the worst possible place to bleed because it sits posteriorly and is unreachable once the prosthesis is seated."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b11-01","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, classifications and the numbers you must own","partId":"block:11","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":10,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, classifications and the numbers you must own","text":"Own the severity thresholds exactly: {{c1::severe AS}} is Vmax 4 m/s or more, mean gradient 40 mmHg or more, AVA 1.0 cm2 or less, or indexed AVA 0.6 cm2/m2 or less; very severe is Vmax 5 m/s or more or mean gradient 60 mmHg or more."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b11-02","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, classifications and the numbers you must own","partId":"block:11","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":10,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, classifications and the numbers you must own","text":"Know patient-prosthesis mismatch thresholds (moderate EOAi under 0.85 cm2/m2, severe under 0.65 cm2/m2) and the three named root-enlargement operations in order of extent — {{c1::Nicks}} (into the noncoronary sinus/aortomitral curtain), Manouguian (between the noncoronary and left coronary sinuses, further into the curtain), and Konno (transventricular, across the septum)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-savr-b11-03","section":"savr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, classifications and the numbers you must own","partId":"block:11","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":10,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, classifications and the numbers you must own","text":"Know the {{c1::Sievers classification}} of bicuspid valve morphology by raphe number (type 0, 1, 2) and that bicuspid disease mandates assessment of the ascending aorta for associated aortopathy."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b01-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Start here — the mediastinal anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the mediastinal anatomy you forgot","text":"The sternum is a flat bone in three parts: the {{c1::manubrium}} above, the body below, joined at the manubriosternal (sternal) angle — the palpable ridge at the level of the second costal cartilage — and the small cartilaginous xiphoid process at the tip."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b01-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Start here — the mediastinal anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the mediastinal anatomy you forgot","text":"Superior to the manubrium, the two clavicular heads are tethered to the sternal notch by the {{c1::interclavicular ligament}}, which must be divided or swept aside to fully expose the notch."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b01-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Start here — the mediastinal anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the mediastinal anatomy you forgot","text":"Inferiorly, the {{c1::xiphoid}} marks where the linea alba begins and is often excised or split to give room at the diaphragmatic end of the incision."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b02-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the midline matters and why the marrow bleeds","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the midline matters and why the marrow bleeds","text":"A cut that wanders off the midline — a {{c1::paramedian}} or oblique cut — leaves one side with a thin sliver of cortical bone at the wire holes."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b02-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the midline matters and why the marrow bleeds","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the midline matters and why the marrow bleeds","text":"That thin fragment is mechanically weak, tends to fracture under the tension of the closure wires, and produces an {{c1::unstable hemisternum}}: a segment that rocks with respiration and coughing, sets up a nidus for wire pull-through, and is a major driver of nonunion, painful clicking, and wound breakdown."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b02-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the midline matters and why the marrow bleeds","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology & pathophysiology — why the midline matters and why the marrow bleeds","text":"The sternum's periosteal and marrow blood supply is shared with the two internal thoracic arteries; harvesting {{c1::both ITAs}} for grafting (bilateral IMA harvest) removes a substantial fraction of that supply and measurably devascularizes the bone, particularly in a sternum whose microvascular healing is already impaired by diabetes (glycosylation-driven microangiopathy and impaired neutrophil function) or mechanically stressed by obesity (higher tension across the closure with fat retracting the wound edges, plus reduced local perfusion in adipose tissue)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b03-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Indications and pre-operative planning — first-time versus hostile re-entry","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Indications and pre-operative planning — first-time versus hostile re-entry","text":"Every redo sternotomy carries a real risk of lacerating a structure adherent to the back of the bone, and the single most useful tool for lowering that risk is a {{c1::preoperative CT chest}} obtained specifically to plan the re-entry."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b03-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Indications and pre-operative planning — first-time versus hostile re-entry","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Indications and pre-operative planning — first-time versus hostile re-entry","text":"The surgeon reads it for the {{c1::distance from the posterior sternal table to the right ventricle, the ascending}} aorta, and any patent bypass graft (a patent vein graft or a LIMA graft can be draped directly beneath the sternum, sometimes touching it) and for how much retrosternal fat or lung has interposed as a protective cushion since the first operation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b03-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Indications and pre-operative planning — first-time versus hostile re-entry","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Indications and pre-operative planning — first-time versus hostile re-entry","text":"The single most important mitigation for a hostile re-entry is {{c1::pre-emptive peripheral cannulation}} — establishing femoral (or axillary) arterial and venous access and, in genuinely dangerous cases, starting cardiopulmonary bypass, or at minimum having the perfusionist primed and the groin prepped and draped, before the sternum is reopened."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b04-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"The operation, step by step — a first-time (primary) sternotomy","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — a first-time (primary) sternotomy","text":"An {{c1::oscillating (sagittal) saw}} is then run down the exact midline from top to bottom in one continuous pass, splitting the manubrium and body cleanly into two symmetric halves; a reciprocating or Gigli (wire) saw is an alternative depending on surgeon preference and, particularly, in reoperative cases."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b04-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"The operation, step by step — a first-time (primary) sternotomy","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — a first-time (primary) sternotomy","text":"Once the sternum is split, the freshly cut marrow edges bleed diffusely and are controlled with {{c1::bone wax}} pressed into the cut cortical surface plus electrocautery to the periosteal edges — this step is done before the retractor is opened, not after, because compressing the bleeding marrow surfaces early minimizes total blood loss."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b04-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"The operation, step by step — a first-time (primary) sternotomy","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — a first-time (primary) sternotomy","text":"A sternal (Finochietto-type) retractor is then inserted and opened {{c1::gradually and symmetrically}} — rushing the spread risks a sternal or costal fracture and, more urgently, can tear a retrosternal structure if the surgeon has not yet confirmed the retrosternal space is free."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b05-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Variations — re-entry technique, alternative cannulation, and alternative access","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Variations — re-entry technique, alternative cannulation, and alternative access","text":"Rather than running a saw blindly through unknown adhesions, many surgeons use a {{c1::reciprocating or oscillating saw with an oscillating (rather than continuously rotating) action}} precisely to reduce soft-tissue injury, or pass a Gigli (wire) saw retrosternally through the old sternal cut line — using the fact that the previous midline is usually still identifiable as the path of least resistance — while a hand or instrument protects the retrosternal space from below if any space can be developed."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b05-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Variations — re-entry technique, alternative cannulation, and alternative access","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Variations — re-entry technique, alternative cannulation, and alternative access","text":"Redo sternotomy safety sequence — \"{{c1::plan, expose, prime, then cut}}\": CT-plan the danger distances, expose peripheral vessels first if hostile, prime perfusion (cannulate or have the perfusionist ready), only then divide the bone."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b05-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Variations — re-entry technique, alternative cannulation, and alternative access","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Variations — re-entry technique, alternative cannulation, and alternative access","text":"A {{c1::redo sternotomy}} follows the same midline principle but adds a deliberate slow-motion phase before the bone is even touched."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b06-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Closure — wires, weaves and plates, and what makes a sternum mechanically stable","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Closure — wires, weaves and plates, and what makes a sternum mechanically stable","text":"The default technique is {{c1::simple peristernal wires}} — heavy stainless-steel wire passed through or around the bone at multiple levels (commonly five to eight passes from manubrium to xiphoid) and twisted down under tension."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b06-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Closure — wires, weaves and plates, and what makes a sternum mechanically stable","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Closure — wires, weaves and plates, and what makes a sternum mechanically stable","text":"In patients with weaker or osteoporotic bone, a wider sternal split, or any concern about a hemisternal fracture, additional patterns are added: {{c1::figure-of-8 wires}} that cross the midline for extra rotational stability, or a Robicsek weave — a continuous longitudinal wire woven in and out along each hemisternal edge like a running whip-stitch before the transverse wires are placed — which distributes load along the whole length of each half rather than concentrating it at discrete wire holes, and is particularly used when a hemisternum is fractured, thin, or osteoporotic."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b06-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Closure — wires, weaves and plates, and what makes a sternum mechanically stable","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Closure — wires, weaves and plates, and what makes a sternum mechanically stable","text":"In high-risk closures — a sternum fractured during re-entry, severe osteoporosis, an obese patient, a planned bilateral ITA harvest, or simply a surgeon's preference to reduce dehiscence risk in a high-risk patient — {{c1::rigid plate fixation}} (titanium plates screwed across the sternal halves, analogous to fracture plating elsewhere in the skeleton) provides substantially more mechanical rigidity than wire alone and is increasingly used primarily in these higher-risk closures."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b07-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"Complications of sternotomy divide naturally into what can go wrong during the cut or re-entry and what can go wrong afterward in the {{c1::closed wound}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b07-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"During a primary sternotomy the main intraoperative risks are marrow bleeding (managed with bone wax and cautery as described above) and, rarely, an unrecognized pleural or pericardial adhesion; during a {{c1::redo sternotomy}} the feared intraoperative complication is direct laceration of the right ventricle, a patent graft, or the aorta by the saw or scissors, which is why the CT-based danger-distance assessment and pre-emptive peripheral cannulation described earlier exist — if it happens, immediate digital or sponge-stick pressure on the injury while the perfusionist establishes bypass through already-prepared femoral vessels is the standard rescue."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b07-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Complications & how to manage them","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Complications & how to manage them","text":"In the healed or healing wound, {{c1::sternal dehiscence}} is mechanical failure of the closure — wires pulling through bone, cutting loose, or a hemisternal fracture propagating — presenting as a clicking or rocking sternum, increasing pain with movement or coughing, and sometimes a visibly widening or draining wound; it is confirmed clinically (a positive \"click\" on gentle sternal compression) or on CT, and stable but symptomatic dehiscence without infection is typically managed with sternal re-wiring or plating, while dehiscence with infection is managed as below."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b08-01","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, evidence and the fine print","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, evidence and the fine print","text":"The {{c1::El Oakley and Wright classification of mediastinitis}} grades by time of onset and risk-factor burden (types I through IV, factoring in early versus late presentation and the presence of risk factors and prior failed treatment), which helps standardize how aggressively to treat and how to counsel prognosis."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b08-02","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, evidence and the fine print","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, evidence and the fine print","text":"Superficial and deep sternal wound infections are also commonly described by the older {{c1::Pairolero classification}} of sternal wound infection into three types by timing and depth of presentation (acute serosanguinous drainage without osteomyelitis; a more delayed presentation with chondritis/osteomyelitis; and a chronic, indolent draining sinus presenting months to years later, sometimes associated with a low-grade wire-related infection)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-sternotomy-b08-03","section":"sternotomy","category":"foundational","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, evidence and the fine print","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, evidence and the fine print","text":"On the evidence for prevention: the {{c1::ART (Arterial Revascularization Trial)}}, designed primarily to compare single versus bilateral internal thoracic artery grafting for long-term survival and graft patency, found that bilateral ITA harvest carries a materially higher risk of deep sternal wound infection than single ITA harvest, an effect that is most pronounced in diabetic and obese patients — this is why skeletonized (rather than pedicled) bilateral harvest, which preserves more of the sternal collateral blood supply, and strict perioperative glycemic control are emphasized whenever bilateral ITA grafting is planned."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b01-01","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — the tricuspid anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the tricuspid anatomy you forgot","text":"It has {{c1::three leaflets}} — the anterior (the largest), the posterior (the smallest), and the septal leaflet (attached along the interventricular septum) — supported by chordae and papillary muscles, including the anterior papillary muscle from which the moderator band runs."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b01-02","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — the tricuspid anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the tricuspid anatomy you forgot","text":"First, the {{c1::atrioventricular (AV) node}} and the penetrating bundle of His lie in the triangle of Koch, bounded by the septal tricuspid annulus, the tendon of Todaro, and the coronary sinus os — so sutures placed in the septal annulus and the anteroseptal commissure can produce complete heart block."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b01-03","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Start here — the tricuspid anatomy you forgot","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the tricuspid anatomy you forgot","text":"Second, the {{c1::right coronary artery}} runs in the right atrioventricular groove close to the anterior and posterior tricuspid annulus and can be caught by a deep annular bite."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b02-01","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — functional versus organic tricuspid regurgitation","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — functional versus organic tricuspid regurgitation","text":"The dominant lesion of the tricuspid valve is regurgitation, and the single most important concept is that most tricuspid regurgitation is {{c1::functional (secondary)}} — the leaflets are structurally normal, but the valve leaks because the annulus has dilated and the right ventricle has enlarged and tethered the leaflets."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b02-02","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — functional versus organic tricuspid regurgitation","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — functional versus organic tricuspid regurgitation","text":"A minority of tricuspid disease is {{c1::organic (primary)}}, where the leaflets themselves are abnormal: infective endocarditis (classically in intravenous drug use, producing large vegetations and leaflet destruction), rheumatic disease (usually with concomitant mitral disease), carcinoid heart disease (serotonin-driven plaque causing thickened, retracted, fixed leaflets and both stenosis and regurgitation), Ebstein anomaly (apical displacement of the septal and posterior leaflets), pacemaker or defibrillator lead interference with leaflet coaptation, blunt trauma, and myxomatous prolapse."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b02-03","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — functional versus organic tricuspid regurgitation","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — functional versus organic tricuspid regurgitation","text":"That is why the workhorse repair is an {{c1::annuloplasty ring}} that restores annular size and shape, and why treating the tricuspid at the time of left-sided surgery prevents late right heart failure."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b03-01","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications","text":"The key measurement that changes management is the {{c1::tricuspid annular diameter}}: a dilated annulus predicts progression even when the current regurgitation is only moderate."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b03-02","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications","text":"Crucially, guidelines also recommend repairing the tricuspid at the time of left-sided surgery when the regurgitation is less than severe but the {{c1::annulus}} is dilated (a tricuspid annular diameter of at least 40 mm, or at least 21 mm per m squared indexed) or when there is right heart failure, because leaving a dilated annulus alone leads to progressive late regurgitation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b03-03","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications","text":"Tricuspid regurgitation is often silent until the right heart fails, when patients present with fatigue, peripheral oedema, ascites, hepatic congestion and a {{c1::raised jugular venous pressure}} with a prominent v wave."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b04-01","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"Tricuspid surgery is done on cardiopulmonary bypass with {{c1::bicaval cannulation and caval snares}}, because the right atrium is opened and the field must be dry."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b04-02","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"It can be performed on the arrested heart with cardioplegia, or on a {{c1::beating (or fibrillating) heart}} with the aorta unclamped — a useful option when the tricuspid is being repaired after the left-sided procedure is complete, because it avoids extra ischaemic time."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b04-03","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"The operation, step by step","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step","text":"For the common functional case the repair is a {{c1::ring annuloplasty}}: an appropriately sized, usually rigid or semi-rigid, prosthetic ring is sewn to the annulus to restore its normal size and three-dimensional shape and to bring the leaflets back into coaptation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b05-01","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Repair techniques and when to replace","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Repair techniques and when to replace","text":"Several repair techniques exist, but they are not equivalent in durability. {{c1::Ring annuloplasty}} — implanting a prosthetic ring or band — is the preferred and most durable repair, and rigid or semi-rigid complete-shaped rings have been shown to give lower recurrence of regurgitation than a simple suture technique."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b05-02","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Repair techniques and when to replace","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Repair techniques and when to replace","text":"The {{c1::De Vega suture annuloplasty}} is a purse-string suture that plicates the anterior and posterior annulus; it is quick and cheap but less durable and more prone to recurrence, and is reserved for selected or resource-limited situations."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b05-03","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Repair techniques and when to replace","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Repair techniques and when to replace","text":"The prosthesis choice at the tricuspid position is distinctive: a {{c1::bioprosthesis}} is generally preferred over a mechanical valve at the tricuspid position, because the low-pressure, low-velocity, sluggish flow through the right heart makes a mechanical tricuspid valve especially prone to thrombosis, and because a bioprosthesis in the low-stress right side tends to be more durable than in the systemic circulation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b06-01","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"The signature complication of tricuspid surgery is {{c1::complete heart block}} from injury to the AV node or His bundle by septal sutures."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b06-02","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"Because the risk is real, functioning pacing wires are placed routinely and rhythm is monitored. {{c1::Recurrent or residual tricuspid regurgitation}} is the commonest longer-term problem, more likely after suture annuloplasty than after a shaped ring and when the underlying right ventricular disease or pulmonary hypertension persists. Right ventricular failure can worsen after the regurgitant \"pop-off\" is removed, particularly with pre-existing RV dysfunction, and needs inotropic and pulmonary-vasodilator support."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b06-03","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Complications and how to manage them","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Complications and how to manage them","text":"For prosthetic replacements, {{c1::valve thrombosis}} is the particular hazard of the low-flow right side (highest with mechanical valves), presenting with a rising gradient and right heart failure and treated by anticoagulation, thrombolysis, or reoperation. Prosthetic or device-lead endocarditis, especially in patients with a history of intravenous drug use, and structural deterioration of bioprostheses over years complete the picture."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b07-01","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named diseases and transcatheter therapy","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named diseases and transcatheter therapy","text":"The {{c1::annular-dilation threshold}} that triggers concomitant tricuspid repair at left-sided surgery even when regurgitation is not severe is a tricuspid annular diameter of at least 40 mm (or at least 21 mm per m squared indexed)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b07-02","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named diseases and transcatheter therapy","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named diseases and transcatheter therapy","text":"Know that {{c1::ring annuloplasty}} is more durable than the De Vega suture, and that a bioprosthesis is preferred over a mechanical valve at the tricuspid because of right-sided thrombosis risk."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-tvr-b07-03","section":"tvr","category":"valve","topic":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named diseases and transcatheter therapy","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — thresholds, named diseases and transcatheter therapy","text":"Know the organic causes and their signatures: {{c1::carcinoid}} (serotonin, plaque, fixed thickened leaflets, right-sided lesions because the lungs clear the vasoactive substances before they reach the left heart, associated with elevated urinary 5-HIAA), Ebstein anomaly (apically displaced septal and posterior leaflets, atrialized right ventricle, association with Wolff-Parkinson-White), endocarditis in intravenous drug use, and pacemaker-lead regurgitation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b01-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the aortic wall and why it splits","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the aortic wall and why it splits","text":"The inner lining is the {{c1::intima}}, a thin endothelial layer; the thick middle layer is the media, made of elastic and smooth-muscle fibres that give the aorta its strength and recoil; and the outer layer is the adventitia, a tough fibrous coat that is often the last thing holding a dissecting aorta together."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b01-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the aortic wall and why it splits","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the aortic wall and why it splits","text":"A {{c1::dissection}} begins when the intima tears, and blood under arterial pressure is driven into the media, splitting it longitudinally along a plane and creating a second, false lumen alongside the original true lumen."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b01-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Start here — the aortic wall and why it splits","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the aortic wall and why it splits","text":"The aorta begins at the {{c1::aortic root}} (annulus, three sinuses of Valsalva, and the origins of the coronary arteries), continues as the ascending aorta, then curves as the arch giving off the innominate (brachiocephalic), left common carotid and left subclavian arteries, before becoming the descending aorta."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b02-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Classification — Stanford, DeBakey and why type A is different","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Classification — Stanford, DeBakey and why type A is different","text":"The {{c1::Stanford}} classification is the one to use reflexively: type A involves the ascending aorta (regardless of where the intimal tear started or how far it extends distally), and type B is confined to the aorta distal to the left subclavian artery, sparing the ascending aorta."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b02-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Classification — Stanford, DeBakey and why type A is different","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Classification — Stanford, DeBakey and why type A is different","text":"The {{c1::DeBakey}} classification is more granular: type I starts in the ascending aorta and extends through the arch and beyond; type II is confined to the ascending aorta; and type III begins in the descending aorta (IIIa above and IIIb below the diaphragm)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b02-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Classification — Stanford, DeBakey and why type A is different","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Classification — Stanford, DeBakey and why type A is different","text":"The distinction is life-or-death because {{c1::any ascending (type A) involvement}} is a surgical emergency, while an uncomplicated type B dissection is generally managed medically."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b03-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"The lethal complications you are racing against","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"The lethal complications you are racing against","text":"The single commonest cause of death is {{c1::rupture into the pericardium producing cardiac tamponade}}: blood fills the pericardial sac, compresses the heart, and stops it filling, causing sudden circulatory collapse."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b03-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"The lethal complications you are racing against","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"The lethal complications you are racing against","text":"The other lethal complications follow the anatomy. {{c1::Acute aortic regurgitation}} occurs when the dissection undermines the commissural attachments of the aortic valve, dropping the leaflets and flooding the left ventricle in diastole, causing acute heart failure. Coronary malperfusion occurs when the flap shears or covers a coronary ostium (most often the right), causing a myocardial infarction and cardiogenic shock. Cerebral malperfusion occurs when the flap compromises an arch vessel, causing stroke."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b03-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"The lethal complications you are racing against","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"The lethal complications you are racing against","text":"A patient with acute type A dissection and a pericardial effusion is developing {{c1::tamponade}}, the leading cause of death."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b04-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, diagnosis and immediate management","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, diagnosis and immediate management","text":"The classic presentation is the sudden onset of severe, {{c1::tearing or ripping chest and back pain}}, often migrating as the dissection propagates, in a patient who is frequently hypertensive or has a connective-tissue disorder."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b04-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, diagnosis and immediate management","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, diagnosis and immediate management","text":"Examination may show a {{c1::pulse or blood-pressure differential between the arms}} (from arch-vessel involvement), a new diastolic murmur of aortic regurgitation, signs of tamponade, or focal neurological deficit."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b04-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Presentation, diagnosis and immediate management","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, diagnosis and immediate management","text":"The definitive investigation in a stable patient is {{c1::CT angiography}} of the whole aorta, which shows the intimal flap, the extent, the true and false lumens, and any malperfusion; in the unstable patient or intraoperatively, transoesophageal echocardiography defines the ascending aorta, the aortic valve, coronary involvement and pericardial blood."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b05-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation, step by step — cannulation and the open distal anastomosis","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — cannulation and the open distal anastomosis","text":"The goal of the operation is to resect the segment of aorta containing the {{c1::intimal tear}} (almost always the ascending aorta) and replace it with a graft, thereby removing the entry into the false lumen, restoring flow to the true lumen, and preventing rupture."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b05-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation, step by step — cannulation and the open distal anastomosis","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — cannulation and the open distal anastomosis","text":"Because cannulating the dissected ascending aorta risks perfusing the false lumen, arterial inflow is established at a site that reliably feeds the {{c1::true lumen}} — commonly the axillary (subclavian) artery (which gives antegrade true-lumen flow and can also be used to perfuse the brain), sometimes the innominate artery or direct true-lumen cannulation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b05-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"The operation, step by step — cannulation and the open distal anastomosis","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — cannulation and the open distal anastomosis","text":"To inspect and repair the arch and sew the distal graft safely, the surgeon uses an {{c1::open distal anastomosis}}: the aortic cross-clamp is removed and the circulation is briefly stopped under hypothermic circulatory arrest, protecting the brain with antegrade cerebral perfusion (through the axillary/innominate route) or retrograde cerebral perfusion, while the distal aorta is opened, the tear inspected, and the graft sewn to healthy tissue with the true and false lumens obliterated distally."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b06-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Managing the aortic root and valve","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Managing the aortic root and valve","text":"In the common situation where the {{c1::aortic root and sinuses}} are of normal size and the valve leaflets are intact but the commissures have been undermined by the dissection, the valve can be preserved by resuspending the commissures — re-securing the detached commissural posts to the aortic wall (often reinforced with pledgets or glue) and sewing the graft to the reconstructed sinotubular junction."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b06-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Managing the aortic root and valve","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Managing the aortic root and valve","text":"When the {{c1::root itself}} is aneurysmal or destroyed, when the dissection has wrecked the sinuses or coronary ostia, or when the patient has a connective-tissue disorder such as Marfan syndrome (in whom the root will continue to dilate), a more extensive proximal operation is required: a root replacement, either a composite valve-graft (Bentall procedure with coronary button reimplantation) or a valve-sparing root replacement in a young patient with normal leaflets."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b06-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Managing the aortic root and valve","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"Managing the aortic root and valve","text":"Having dealt with the {{c1::distal aorta}}, the surgeon addresses the proximal end at the root, where the strategy depends on how much the dissection has damaged the root and valve."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b07-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Malperfusion — the problem that persists after the central repair","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Malperfusion — the problem that persists after the central repair","text":"It comes in two mechanistic forms that behave differently. {{c1::Dynamic}} malperfusion is caused by the mobile intimal flap prolapsing across a branch origin like a curtain, and it often improves once the true lumen is re-pressurized by the central repair, because restoring true-lumen flow lifts the flap off the branch. Static malperfusion is caused by the dissection extending into the branch itself and thrombosing or fixedly narrowing it, and it does not resolve with the central repair because the obstruction is within the branch."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b07-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Malperfusion — the problem that persists after the central repair","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Malperfusion — the problem that persists after the central repair","text":"If malperfusion of a vital territory persists — for example, ongoing mesenteric ischaemia with rising lactate, or a pulseless malperfused limb — a {{c1::static}} obstruction is likely and needs a directed intervention on that branch (surgical or endovascular fenestration/stenting) urgently."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b07-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Malperfusion — the problem that persists after the central repair","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Malperfusion — the problem that persists after the central repair","text":"If organ malperfusion (rising lactate, oliguria, a cold pulseless limb, ongoing neurological deficit) persists after a technically successful ascending repair, suspect a {{c1::static}} branch obstruction that the central repair could not relieve, and act on that branch urgently."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b08-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications, postoperative care and the residual dissection","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications, postoperative care and the residual dissection","text":"The early complications of the operation are {{c1::bleeding and coagulopathy}} (from the friable dissected tissue, long bypass, hypothermia and circulatory arrest — meticulous suture-line haemostasis and blood-product support are essential), persistent malperfusion, stroke (embolic or from cerebral malperfusion or the arrest itself), myocardial dysfunction from coronary involvement, renal failure, and the need for reoperation for bleeding."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b08-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications, postoperative care and the residual dissection","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications, postoperative care and the residual dissection","text":"In DeBakey I disease the false lumen frequently persists in the arch and descending aorta beyond the graft — a {{c1::chronic residual dissection}} — which can dilate over years into an aneurysm and rupture."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b08-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Complications, postoperative care and the residual dissection","partId":"block:08","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":7,"sourceTitle":"Complications, postoperative care and the residual dissection","text":"These patients therefore need {{c1::lifelong surveillance}} with serial cross-sectional imaging and strict blood-pressure control (the same anti-impulse principle, keeping heart rate and pressure down to reduce wall stress) to slow expansion of the residual dissected aorta, with reintervention if it reaches operative size."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b09-01","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, thresholds and named strategies","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, thresholds and named strategies","text":"Hold the frameworks cold. {{c1::Stanford A = ascending involved = emergency surgery; Stanford B = distal}} to the left subclavian = usually medical. DeBakey I (ascending + arch + beyond), II (ascending only), III (descending)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b09-02","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, thresholds and named strategies","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, thresholds and named strategies","text":"The {{c1::Penn classification}} stratifies by ischaemia (Aa none, Ab branch malperfusion, Ac circulatory collapse, Abc both)."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-type-a-b09-03","section":"type-a","category":"aorta","topic":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, thresholds and named strategies","partId":"block:09","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":8,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — classifications, thresholds and named strategies","text":"Know that the leading cause of death is {{c1::tamponade}}, that untreated type A mortality rises roughly 1 to 2 percent per hour early, and that pericardiocentesis is generally avoided in favour of emergency surgery."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b01-01","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Start here — the atrial anatomy behind the arrhythmia","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the atrial anatomy behind the arrhythmia","text":"On the anterolateral surface of the left atrium sits the {{c1::left atrial appendage}}, a trabeculated blind pouch that is the single most important site of thrombus formation in this disease."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b01-02","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Start here — the atrial anatomy behind the arrhythmia","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the atrial anatomy behind the arrhythmia","text":"Between the left superior pulmonary vein and the left pulmonary artery runs a fold of tissue carrying the {{c1::ligament of Marshall}}, the remnant of the embryonic left superior vena cava."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b01-03","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Start here — the atrial anatomy behind the arrhythmia","partId":"block:01","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":0,"sourceTitle":"Start here — the atrial anatomy behind the arrhythmia","text":"Between the inferior vena caval orifice and the tricuspid annulus, floored by the Eustachian valve and the coronary sinus, lies the {{c1::cavotricuspid isthmus}}, the obligatory limb of typical atrial flutter."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b02-01","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — triggers, substrate and the two mechanisms","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — triggers, substrate and the two mechanisms","text":"Atrial fibrillation is defined as uncoordinated atrial activity at a rate of {{c1::300 to 500 beats per minute}}, with the ventricular response determined by whatever the atrioventricular node happens to conduct."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b02-02","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — triggers, substrate and the two mechanisms","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — triggers, substrate and the two mechanisms","text":"Paroxysmal AF is driven mainly by the initiating focal automaticity around the pulmonary veins, so it can be treated by {{c1::pulmonary vein isolation}} alone."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b02-03","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Physiology and pathophysiology — triggers, substrate and the two mechanisms","partId":"block:02","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":1,"sourceTitle":"Physiology and pathophysiology — triggers, substrate and the two mechanisms","text":"The lost atrial kick produces a roughly {{c1::30 percent}} loss of cardiac output, which over time can produce a tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b03-01","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications","text":"A direct oral anticoagulant is preferred over a vitamin K antagonist, except for mechanical heart valves and moderate-to-severe mitral stenosis, where {{c1::warfarin}} remains the drug of choice."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b03-02","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications","text":"Two features predict failure and must be sought explicitly: a left atrium larger than {{c1::5 cm}} and atrial fibrillation lasting more than five years."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b03-03","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Presentation, workup and indications","partId":"block:03","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":2,"sourceTitle":"Presentation, workup and indications","text":"The CHA2DS2-VASc score awards two points each for age 75 or over and for {{c1::prior stroke, TIA or thromboembolism}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b04-01","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"The operation, step by step — the Cox-Maze lesion set","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — the Cox-Maze lesion set","text":"A maze is a set of blind alleys with a single entrance and a single exit, forcing atrial activation along one route from the sino-atrial node to the {{c1::atrioventricular node}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b04-02","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"The operation, step by step — the Cox-Maze lesion set","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — the Cox-Maze lesion set","text":"The left-sided set is completed by a lesion running from the left atriotomy down to the {{c1::P2/P3 region of the posterior mitral annulus}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b04-03","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"The operation, step by step — the Cox-Maze lesion set","partId":"block:04","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":3,"sourceTitle":"The operation, step by step — the Cox-Maze lesion set","text":"The right atrial lesions reach the superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava, the 2 o'clock position of the tricuspid annulus, and from the right atrial appendage the {{c1::10 o'clock position}} of that annulus."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b05-01","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Energy sources and choosing the lesion set","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Energy sources and choosing the lesion set","text":"Surgical radiofrequency ablation uses an alternating current at 350 kHz to 1 MHz to heat tissue to {{c1::70 to 80 degrees Celsius}} for about one minute."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b05-02","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Energy sources and choosing the lesion set","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Energy sources and choosing the lesion set","text":"Cryoablation uses {{c1::nitrous oxide}} as the cooling agent at about minus 60 degrees Celsius for two minutes, and the surgeon can watch the iceball form."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b05-03","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Energy sources and choosing the lesion set","partId":"block:05","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":4,"sourceTitle":"Energy sources and choosing the lesion set","text":"For surgeons using bipolar radiofrequency, lesions approaching the atrioventricular grooves are made with {{c1::cryoablation}} instead, to avoid thermal injury to the coronary vessels."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b06-01","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"The left atrial appendage","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The left atrial appendage","text":"In patients with atrial fibrillation who go on to suffer a stroke, the left atrial appendage is the site of thrombus in up to {{c1::90 percent}} of cases."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b06-02","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"The left atrial appendage","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The left atrial appendage","text":"LAAOS III randomized 4770 cardiac surgical patients and at a mean follow-up of 3.8 years reported fewer strokes or systemic emboli with occlusion, {{c1::4.8 versus 7.0 percent}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b06-03","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"The left atrial appendage","partId":"block:06","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":5,"sourceTitle":"The left atrial appendage","text":"Excluding the appendage by internal suturing proves incomplete in up to {{c1::50 percent}} of cases, and the residual small-orifice communication may itself raise the risk of thrombus formation."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b07-01","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Pimping ceiling — complications, outcomes and atrial flutter","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — complications, outcomes and atrial flutter","text":"Transvenous catheter ablation carries roughly a 60 percent success rate, whereas surgical ablation achieves roughly {{c1::75 to 80 percent}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b07-02","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Pimping ceiling — complications, outcomes and atrial flutter","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — complications, outcomes and atrial flutter","text":"Injury to the node or its artery at the superior cavoatrial junction gives a junctional rhythm or slow escape and a meaningful rate of {{c1::permanent pacemaker implantation}}."},
  {"id":"sxcomp-maze-b07-03","section":"maze","category":"rhythm","topic":"Pimping ceiling — complications, outcomes and atrial flutter","partId":"block:07","sourceKind":"block","sourceIndex":6,"sourceTitle":"Pimping ceiling — complications, outcomes and atrial flutter","text":"Typical atrial flutter arises from a {{c1::single macro re-entrant circuit}}, not the multiple circuits of fibrillation, which is why the rhythm stays regular."},
);
